{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06198","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06198","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06198","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengliang","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Zhengliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minglai","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Minglai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mahdi","familyName":"Rahimi","name":"Rahimi, Mahdi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mihai","familyName":"Surdeanu","name":"Surdeanu, Mihai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Answer Lies Within: Self-Derived Rewards Enable Explainable Relation Extraction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:53:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:03:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-16T14:42:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-17T02:38:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-13T04:01:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-16T00:20:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-21T08:05:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-10-22T00:45:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite the remarkable reasoning capabilities of large language models, they still struggle with one-shot relation extraction without predefined relation labels. We identify two pitfalls: models are often misled by irrelevant tokens instead of relation-conveying semantics, and they often fail to align with the abstraction level human annotators expect. We introduce a novel framework that closes this gap with two components: (1) COGRE, a cognitively-inspired reasoning framework that structures RE into a series of processes mimicking human text-processing; and (2) HIT@DICT, a reinforcement learning intermediate reward strategy that encourages reasoning to align with relational labels by rewarding relation-relevant phrases in reasoning. The reward is derived on a credit dictionary automatically extracted from correct predictions. Our experiments show that our framework improves both accuracy and explanation quality by addressing these two pitfalls. For example, COGRE with Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct on One-shot NYT29 achieves 24.65% F1, surpassing prior reasoning-based designs. Optimizing this approach with RL using HIT@DICT further improves performance by +23.46% points. Finally, human evaluation shows that our best model generates relational phrases closely aligned with gold labels, increasing human explanation quality ratings by 54% (relative)."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06198","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:06:40Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:06:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-22T03:00:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05935","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05935","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05935","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamed","familyName":"Bal-Ghaoui","name":"Bal-Ghaoui, Mohamed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fayssal","familyName":"Sabri","name":"Sabri, Fayssal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM-FS-Agent: A Deliberative Role-based Large Language Model Architecture for Transparent Feature Selection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T13:46:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-14T02:25:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.11591/ijai.v15.i5.pp4026-4038","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"High-dimensional data remains a pervasive challenge in machine learning, often undermining model interpretability and computational efficiency. While Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown promise for dimensionality reduction through feature selection, existing LLM-based approaches frequently lack structured reasoning and transparent justification for their decisions. This paper introduces LLM-FS-Agent, a novel multi-agent architecture designed for interpretable and robust feature selection. The system orchestrates a deliberative \"debate\" among multiple LLM agents, each assigned a specific role, enabling collective evaluation of feature relevance and generation of detailed justifications. We evaluate LLM-FS-Agent in the cybersecurity domain using the CIC-DIAD 2024 IoT intrusion detection dataset and compare its performance against strong baselines, including LLM-Select and traditional methods such as PCA. Experimental results demonstrate that LLM-FS-Agent consistently achieves superior or comparable classification performance while reducing downstream training time by an average of 46% (statistically significant improvement, p = 0.028 for XGBoost). These findings highlight that the proposed deliberative architecture enhances both decision transparency and computational efficiency, establishing LLM-FS-Agent as a practical and reliable solution for real-world applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05935","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-08T02:00:30Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:00:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:20:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05544","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05544","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05544","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryan","familyName":"Solgi","name":"Solgi, Ryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Parsa","familyName":"Madinei","name":"Madinei, Parsa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayi","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Jiayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rupak","familyName":"Swaminathan","name":"Swaminathan, Rupak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nathan","familyName":"Susanj","name":"Susanj, Nathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Activation-Informed Pareto-Guided Low-Rank Compression for Efficient LLM/VLM"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T03:07:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:21:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-03T18:20:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-05T00:06:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-06T20:51:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-08T00:13:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLM) and vision-language models (VLM) have achieved state-of-the-art performance, but they impose significant memory and computing challenges in deployment. We present a novel low-rank compression framework to address this challenge. First, we upper bound the change of network loss via layer-wise activation-based compression errors, filling a theoretical gap in the literature. We then formulate low-rank model compression as a bi-objective optimization and prove that a single uniform tolerance yields surrogate Pareto-optimal heterogeneous ranks. Based on our theoretical insights, we propose Pareto-Guided Singular Value Decomposition (PGSVD), a zero-shot pipeline that improves activation-aware compression via Pareto-guided rank selection and alternating least-squares implementation. We apply PGSVD to both LLM and VLM, showing better accuracy at the same compression levels and inference speedup."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05544","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:51:20Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:51:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-08T03:03:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04852","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04852","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04852","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Victor","familyName":"May","name":"May, Victor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diganta","familyName":"Misra","name":"Misra, Diganta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanqi","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Yanqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anjali","familyName":"Sridhar","name":"Sridhar, Anjali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Justine","familyName":"Gehring","name":"Gehring, Justine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Silvio Soares Ribeiro","familyName":"Junior","name":"Junior, Silvio Soares Ribeiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FreshBrew: A Benchmark for Evaluating AI Agents on Java Code Migration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T14:39:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:37:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:55:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T01:19:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-03T01:00:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-06T00:25:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3744916.3773214","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"AI coding assistants are rapidly becoming integral to modern software development. A key challenge in this space is the continual need to migrate and modernize codebases in response to evolving software ecosystems. Traditionally, such migrations have relied on rule-based systems and human intervention. With the advent of powerful large language models (LLMs), AI-driven agentic frameworks offer a promising alternative-but their effectiveness has not been systematically evaluated. In this paper, we introduce \\FreshBrew{}\\footnote{\\url{https://github.com/mrcabbage972/freshbrew}}, a novel benchmark for evaluating AI agents on project-level Java migrations, with a specific focus on measuring an agent's ability to preserve program semantics and avoid reward hacking, which we argue requires projects with high test coverage for a rigorous and reliable evaluation. We benchmark several state-of-the-art LLMs, and compare their performance against established rule-based tools. Our evaluation on a challenging subset of 228 public Maven repositories that build on JDK 8, fail on JDK 17, and have high test coverage shows that the top-performing model, Gemini 2.5 Flash, can successfully migrate 52.3\\% of projects to JDK 17. Our empirical analysis reveals novel insights into the critical strengths and limitations of current agentic approaches, offering actionable insights into their real-world applicability. Our empirical study reveals failure modes of current AI agents in realistic Java modernization tasks, providing a foundation for evaluating trustworthy code-migration systems. By releasing \\FreshBrew{}, we aim to facilitate rigorous, reproducible evaluation and catalyze progress in AI-driven codebase modernization."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 12 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04852","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T16:56:02Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:56:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-06T05:42:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06105","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06105","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06105","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Batu","familyName":"El","name":"El, Batu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Moloch's Bargain: Emergent Misalignment When LLMs Compete for Audiences"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T16:37:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:58:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-01T08:09:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-02T00:54:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly shaping how information is created and disseminated, from companies using them to craft persuasive advertisements, to election campaigns optimizing messaging to gain votes, to social media influencers boosting engagement. These settings are inherently competitive, with sellers, candidates, and influencers vying for audience approval, yet it remains poorly understood how competitive feedback loops influence LLM behavior. We show that optimizing LLMs for competitive success can inadvertently drive misalignment. Using simulated environments across these scenarios, we find that, 6.3% increase in sales is accompanied by a 14.0% rise in deceptive marketing; in elections, a 4.9% gain in vote share coincides with 22.3% more disinformation and 12.5% more populist rhetoric; and on social media, a 7.5% engagement boost comes with 188.6% more disinformation and a 16.3% increase in promotion of harmful behaviors. We call this phenomenon Moloch's Bargain for AI--competitive success achieved at the cost of alignment. These misaligned behaviors emerge even when models are explicitly instructed to remain truthful and grounded, revealing the fragility of current alignment safeguards. Our findings highlight how market-driven optimization pressures can systematically erode alignment, creating a race to the bottom, and suggest that safe deployment of AI systems will require stronger governance and carefully designed incentives to prevent competitive dynamics from undermining societal trust."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06105","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:04:29Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:04:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-02T03:28:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06128","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06128","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06128","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Dehan Al","familyName":"Kautsar","name":"Kautsar, Muhammad Dehan Al","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fajri","familyName":"Koto","name":"Koto, Fajri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Parallel Tokenizers: Rethinking Vocabulary Design in Cross-Lingual Transfer of Low-Resource Languages"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:05:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:59:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-27T08:12:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-28T01:20:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-26T14:48:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-29T00:52:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Tokenization forms the basis of multilingual language models, yet existing methods often limit cross-lingual transfer by mapping semantically equivalent words to different embeddings. For example, 'I eat rice' in English and 'Ina cin shinkafa' in Hausa are typically mapped to different vocabulary indices, preventing shared representations and limiting cross-lingual generalization. This problem is even more pronounced in low-resource languages, where shared representations could offer the greatest benefit. We introduce parallel tokenizers, a new framework that first trains tokenizers monolingually and then aligns their vocabularies exhaustively using bilingual dictionaries or word-to-word translation. This alignment enforces a shared semantic space across languages while naturally improving fertility balance. To assess their effectiveness, we pretrain a transformer encoder from scratch on thirteen low-resource languages and evaluate it on sentiment analysis, hate speech detection, emotion classification, and sentence embedding similarity. Across all tasks, models trained with parallel tokenizers outperform conventional multilingual baselines, confirming that rethinking tokenization is essential for advancing multilingual representation learning--especially in low-resource settings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at EMNLP 2026 Main (Oral Presentation). 17 pages, 25 tables, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06128","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:05:00Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:05:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-29T06:56:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06042","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06042","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06042","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shang","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Shang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xusheng","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Xusheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanfang","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Yanfang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Agent+P: Guiding UI Agents via Symbolic Planning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:36:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:55:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-07T20:06:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-09T01:05:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-27T17:25:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-29T01:46:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Model (LLM)-based UI agents show great promise for UI automation but often hallucinate in long-horizon tasks due to their lack of understanding of the global UI transition structure. To address this, we introduce AGENT+P, a novel framework that leverages symbolic planning to guide LLM-based UI agents. Specifically, we model an app's UI transition structure as a UI Transition Graph (UTG), which allows us to reformulate the UI automation task as a pathfinding problem on the UTG. This further enables an off-the-shelf symbolic planner to generate a provably correct and optimal high-level plan, preventing the agent from redundant exploration and guiding the agent to achieve the automation goals. AGENT+P is designed as a plug-and-play framework to enhance existing UI agents. Evaluation on the AndroidWorld benchmark demonstrates that AGENT+P improves the success rates of state-of-the-art UI agents by up to 14.32% and reduces the action steps by 37.70%. Our code is available at: https://github.com/KiteFlyKid/agentp."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06042","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:03:01Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:03:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-29T06:56:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05632","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05632","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05632","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianhao","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Tianhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dahu","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Dahu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erhu","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Erhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yubin","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Yubin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Systematic Exploration of Multi-core Architectures for Efficient LLM Serving using WaferAI-SIM"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T07:29:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:29:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-28T12:18:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-29T02:52:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/tcad.2026.3719164","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the widespread adoption of Large Language Models (LLMs), the demand for high-performance LLM inference services continues to grow. Multi-core AI accelerators, such as Groq, Graphcore IPU, and Cerebras WSE, provide promising platforms for LLM serving, but their distributed memory systems require careful coordination between hardware configuration and serving policies. Otherwise, mismatched tensor partitioning, data placement, and memory management can substantially underutilize compute and communication resources.\n To address these challenges, we present WaferAI-SIM, a multi-level simulation framework that combines transaction-level simulation with an analytical performance model. WaferAI-SIM enables simulator-driven co-design of LLM serving strategies and multi-core accelerator architectures, targeting the early design stage in which emerging platforms are not yet broadly available for empirical serving studies. It captures how LLM serving policies interact with compute-core count, memory hierarchy, and interconnect topology, enabling architecture-aware exploration beyond GPU-centric assumptions. We evaluate representative LLMs across a range of chip configurations and serving scenarios. Across the evaluation, WaferAI-SIM reports 1.32$\\times$--6.03$\\times$ latency improvements, where the lower endpoint comes from ring-based placement at TP=16 over the placement baselines, and the upper endpoint comes from K-dimension TP over MN-dimension TP for Qwen3\\_4B at TP=4 with sequence length 256. For LLM serving, our findings provide guidance for co-designing hardware architectures and serving strategies for multi-core AI accelerators across diverse LLM workloads."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05632","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-08T01:53:23Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:53:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-29T06:56:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06093","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06093","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06093","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mallika","familyName":"Mainali","name":"Mainali, Mallika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harsha","familyName":"Sureshbabu","name":"Sureshbabu, Harsha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anik","familyName":"Sen","name":"Sen, Anik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher B.","familyName":"Rauch","name":"Rauch, Christopher B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Noah D.","familyName":"Reifsnyder","name":"Reifsnyder, Noah D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John","familyName":"Meyer","name":"Meyer, John","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J. T.","familyName":"Turner","name":"Turner, J. T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael W.","familyName":"Floyd","name":"Floyd, Michael W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew","familyName":"Molineaux","name":"Molineaux, Matthew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rosina O.","familyName":"Weber","name":"Weber, Rosina O.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Classical AI vs. LLMs for Decision-Maker Alignment in Health Insurance Choices"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T16:21:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-25T01:09:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As algorithmic decision-makers are increasingly applied to high-stakes domains, AI alignment research has evolved from a focus on universal value alignment to context-specific approaches that account for decision-maker attributes. Prior work on Decision-Maker Alignment (DMA) has explored two primary strategies: (1) classical AI methods integrating case-based reasoning, Bayesian reasoning, and naturalistic decision-making, and (2) large language model (LLM)-based methods leveraging prompt engineering. While both approaches have shown promise in limited domains such as medical triage, their generalizability to novel contexts remains underexplored. In this work, we implement a prior classical AI model and develop an LLM-based algorithmic decision-maker evaluated using a large reasoning model (GPT-5) and a non-reasoning model (GPT-4) with weighted self-consistency under a zero-shot prompting framework, as proposed in recent literature. We evaluate both approaches on a health insurance decision-making dataset annotated for three target decision-makers with varying levels of risk tolerance (0.0, 0.5, 1.0). In the experiments reported herein, classical AI and LLM-based models achieved comparable alignment with attribute-based targets, with classical AI exhibiting slightly better alignment for a moderate risk profile. The dataset and open-source implementation are publicly available at: https://github.com/TeX-Base/ClassicalAIvsLLMsforDMAlignment and https://github.com/Parallax-Advanced-Research/ITM/tree/feature_insurance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 3 figures. Accepted at the Twelfth Annual Conference on Advances in Cognitive Systems (ACS 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06093","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:04:13Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:04:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-25T02:51:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05124","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05124","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05124","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingjin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Mingjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huayi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Huayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongguang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hongguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Ruan","name":"Ruan, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MADS: Multi-Agent Dialogue Simulation for Diverse Persuasion Data Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T06:55:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T18:12:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T02:50:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:18:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-09T13:48:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-10T00:51:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose MADS (Multi-Agent Dialogue Simulation), a scalable framework for generating persuasive multi-turn dialogues via agent self-play. MADS employs three coordinated agents: User Agents designed to simulate diverse persona-driven behaviors by leveraging personality signifiers such as Zodiac Signs and MBTI types, a Dialog Agent executing task-oriented persuasion strategies and an Optimization Agent evaluating and refining dialogue outcomes. We further validate its effectiveness through users' Chain-of-Attitude (CoA) modeling and dedicated LLMs' persuasion assessment. This approach enables low-cost generation of training data without human annotation, addressing key industry challenges such as lack of user data, cold-start evaluation difficulties, and prompt inefficiency. Applied to a real-world marketing scenario, MADS significantly improved the persuasion capacity of small LLMs, increasing the organic traffic conversion rate by 22.4% (from 1.83% to 2.24%) , demonstrating clear business value."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EMNLP 2025 Industry Track (https://aclanthology.org/2025.emnlp-industry.26.pdf)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05124","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:41:38Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:41:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-10T02:28:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05364","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05364","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05364","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander M.","familyName":"Fichtl","name":"Fichtl, Alexander M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeremias","familyName":"Bohn","name":"Bohn, Jeremias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Josefin","familyName":"Kelber","name":"Kelber, Josefin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Edoardo","familyName":"Mosca","name":"Mosca, Edoardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Georg","familyName":"Groh","name":"Groh, Georg","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The End of Transformers? On Challenging Attention and the Rise of Sub-Quadratic Architectures"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T20:45:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-02T01:19:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.18653/v1/2026.bigpicture-main.6","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformers have dominated sequence processing tasks for the past seven years -- most notably language modeling. However, the inherent quadratic complexity of their attention mechanism remains a significant bottleneck as context length increases. This paper surveys recent efforts to overcome this bottleneck, including advances in (sub-quadratic) attention variants, recurrent neural networks, state space models, and hybrid architectures. We critically analyze these approaches in terms of compute and memory complexity, benchmark results, and fundamental limitations to assess whether the dominance of pure-attention transformers may soon be challenged."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"21 pages, 2 figures, 2 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05364","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-08T01:47:13Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:47:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-02T02:59:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05291","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05291","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05291","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tarek","familyName":"Naous","name":"Naous, Tarek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anagha","familyName":"Savit","name":"Savit, Anagha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carlos Rafael","familyName":"Catalan","name":"Catalan, Carlos Rafael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Geyang","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Geyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaehyeok","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jaehyeok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyungdon","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Kyungdon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lheane Marie","familyName":"Dizon","name":"Dizon, Lheane Marie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengyu","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Mengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Neel","familyName":"Kothari","name":"Kothari, Neel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sahajpreet","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Sahajpreet","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sarah","familyName":"Masud","name":"Masud, Sarah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tanish","familyName":"Patwa","name":"Patwa, Tanish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Trung Thanh","familyName":"Tran","name":"Tran, Trung Thanh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zohaib","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Zohaib","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alan","familyName":"Ritter","name":"Ritter, Alan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tanmoy","familyName":"Chakraborty","name":"Chakraborty, Tanmoy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuki","familyName":"Arase","name":"Arase, Yuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keisuke","familyName":"Sakaguchi","name":"Sakaguchi, Keisuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"JinYeong","familyName":"Bak","name":"Bak, JinYeong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Camellia: Benchmarking Cultural Biases in LLMs for Asian Languages"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T18:59:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:06:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-27T02:09:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-28T00:26:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-31T21:22:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-02T00:14:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As Large Language Models (LLMs) develop stronger multilingual capabilities, their sensitivity to culturally diverse entities becomes increasingly important. Prior work by Naous et al. (2024) has shown that LLMs often favor Western-associated entities in Arabic. Due to the lack of entity-centric multilingual benchmarks, it remains unclear if such biases also manifest in various non-Western languages. In this paper, we introduce Camellia, a benchmark for evaluating entity-centric cultural biases in nine Asian languages, spanning six Asian cultures. Camellia includes 19,530 manually annotated entities associated with the covered Asian or Western cultures, as well as 2,173 masked contexts for these entities derived from social media posts. Using Camellia, we evaluate cultural biases in four recent multilingual LLMs across three tasks: cultural context adaptation, sentiment association, and entity extractive QA. Our analyses show that LLMs struggle with cultural adaptation across these languages, with performance differing across models developed in different regions. We further observe that different LLM families can hold distinct biases, reflected in the ways they link cultures to particular sentiments. Lastly, we find that LLMs can struggle with context understanding in some Asian languages, creating performance gaps between cultures in entity extraction."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at EMNLP 2026 Main Conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05291","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:45:32Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:45:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-02T02:59:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05545","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05545","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05545","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinrui","familyName":"Ruan","name":"Ruan, Xinrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinwei","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Xinwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingfei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yingfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Waverly","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Waverly","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingshen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jingshen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Can Language Models Boost the Power of Randomized Experiments Without Statistical Bias?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Econometrics (econ.EM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T03:14:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:21:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-06T22:18:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-09T01:38:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-24T00:09:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-25T01:09:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Randomized controlled trials (RCTs) are widely adopted for causal inference, yet cost and sample-size constraints limit power. We introduce CALM (Causal Analysis leveraging Language Models), a statistical framework that integrates insights generated by large language models (LLMs) into the analysis of RCTs using established causal estimators to increase precision while preserving statistical validity. In particular, CALM treats LLM-generated outputs as auxiliary prognostic information and corrects their potential bias via a heterogeneous calibration step that residualizes and optimally reweights predictions. We prove that CALM remains consistent even when LLM predictions are biased and achieves efficiency gains over augmented inverse probability weighting estimators for various causal estimands. In particular, CALM develops a few-shot variant that aggregates predictions across randomly sampled demonstration sets. The resulting U-statistic-like predictor restores i.i.d. structure and also mitigates prompt-selection variability. Empirically, in simulations calibrated to a mobile-app depression RCT, CALM delivers lower variance relative to other benchmarking methods, is effective in zero- and few-shot settings, and remains stable across prompt designs. By principled use of LLMs to harness unstructured data and external knowledge learned during pretraining, CALM provides a practical path to more precise causal analyses."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05545","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:51:21Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:51:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T03:50:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06039","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06039","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06039","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengwei","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Chengwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingrui","familyName":"Zhuo","name":"Zhuo, Xingrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyang","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Mingyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinghe","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Xinghe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhichao","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Zhichao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiapu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiapu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Large-Scale Chinese Knowledge Graph-Text Alignment Dataset for Benchmarking Knowledge-Grounded LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:33:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:55:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-16T14:06:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-18T00:56:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reliable evaluation of knowledge-grounded Large Language Models (LLMs) in Chinese requires resources that explicitly align Chinese-language text with verifiable Knowledge Graph (KG) facts. Yet existing Chinese benchmarks primarily assess general language understanding and offer limited support for structured reasoning under Chinese-specific linguistic phenomena. We introduce the Chinese Data-Text Pair (CDTP), a large-scale Chinese KG-text alignment dataset comprising more than 7 million aligned instances across four broad domains. Each instance pairs a Chinese-language text with one or more textually supported KG triples, totaling 15 million triples. A multi-stage construction pipeline combining alignment filtering, manual verification, and external evidence validation improves semantic consistency and factual reliability. CDTP supports Knowledge Graph Completion (KGC), Question Answering (QA), and Triple-to-Text Generation (T2T). Across all three tasks, the benchmark design accounts for Chinese-specific phenomena, including polysemy, word-segmentation ambiguity, and context-dependent entity interpretation, enabling the evaluation of structured reasoning, ambiguity-aware factual understanding, and knowledge-grounded generation. Experiments with diverse open-source and proprietary LLMs show that model scale alone does not guarantee reliable performance on these Chinese knowledge-intensive tasks, whereas supervised fine-tuning on CDTP consistently improves in-domain performance and out-of-distribution robustness. The publicly accessible dataset, code, and evaluation protocols provide a reusable resource for developing and evaluating knowledge-grounded LLMs in Chinese."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06039","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:02:57Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:02:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-18T03:56:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05740","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05740","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05740","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amirtaha","familyName":"Amanzadi","name":"Amanzadi, Amirtaha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zahra","familyName":"Dehghanian","name":"Dehghanian, Zahra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hamid","familyName":"Beigy","name":"Beigy, Hamid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hamid R.","familyName":"Rabiee","name":"Rabiee, Hamid R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Redefining Generalization in Visual Domains: A Two-Axis Framework for Fake Image Detection with FusionDetect"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T10:01:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:38:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-14T15:50:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-17T00:49:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid development of generative models has made it increasingly crucial to develop detectors that can reliably detect synthetic images. Although most of the work has now focused on cross-generator generalization, we argue that this viewpoint is too limited. Detecting synthetic images involves another equally important challenge: generalization across visual domains. To bridge this gap,we present the OmniGen Benchmark. This comprehensive evaluation dataset incorporates 12 state-of-the-art generators, providing a more realistic way of evaluating detector performance under realistic conditions. In addition, we introduce a new method, FusionDetect, aimed at addressing both vectors of generalization. FusionDetect draws on the benefits of two frozen foundation models: CLIP \u0026amp; Dinov2. By deriving features from both complementary models,we develop a cohesive feature space that naturally adapts to changes in both thecontent and design of the generator. Our extensive experiments demonstrate that FusionDetect delivers not only a new state-of-the-art, which is 3.87% more accurate than its closest competitor and 6.13% more precise on average on established benchmarks, but also achieves a 4.48% increase in accuracy on OmniGen,along with exceptional robustness to common image perturbations. We introduce not only a top-performing detector, but also a new benchmark and framework for furthering universal AI image detection. The code and dataset are available at http://github.com/amir-aman/FusionDetect"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project code: http://github.com/amir-aman/FusionDetect"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05740","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:55:54Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:55:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-17T02:05:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06200","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06200","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06200","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weijian","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Weijian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong-Yu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hong-Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nabeel","familyName":"Rehemtulla","name":"Rehemtulla, Nabeel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ved G.","familyName":"Shah","name":"Shah, Ved G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongho","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Dongho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dennis","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Dennis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinjie","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Qinjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam A.","familyName":"Miller","name":"Miller, Adam A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"StarEmbed: Benchmarking Time Series Foundation Models on Astronomical Observations of Variable Stars"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Solar and Stellar Astrophysics (astro-ph.SR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Instrumentation and Methods for Astrophysics (astro-ph.IM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:53:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:03:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-23T07:49:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-26T01:22:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-18T07:33:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-19T01:26:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-13T04:33:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-08-14T00:24:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current time series foundation model (TSFM) training corpora largely omit data with certain complexities like irregular temporal sampling. Astronomical time series of stellar fluxes (light curves) are available in immense quantities and exhibit irregular sampling, multiple variates, and heteroskedasticity. We introduce StarEmbed, the first public benchmark for light curves comprised of real observations of ~40,000 stars expert-labeled across seven classes and evaluations in clustering, classification, and out-of-distribution (OOD) source detection. We benchmark TSFMs with differing architecture and training strategies as well as domain-specific transformers. Our results demonstrate that the Chronos family, despite being pre-trained on regularly sampled non-astronomical data, yields state-of-the-art (SOTA) performance in light curve clustering and OOD detection. While no TSFM strictly surpasses the classification performance of the long-established domain baseline, they do demonstrate excellent generalization abilities. StarEmbed marks a step toward universal light curve embeddings and improved TSFM performance on challenging data."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06200","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:06:43Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:06:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-14T02:22:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05678","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05678","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05678","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haneul","familyName":"Yoo","name":"Yoo, Haneul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiho","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Jiho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyunghyun","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Kyunghyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alice","familyName":"Oh","name":"Oh, Alice","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Gradual Code-Switching as Inference-Time Cross-Lingual Representational Alignment for LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T08:35:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:33:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-13T12:53:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-14T00:47:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While large language models (LLMs) have achieved notable progress in multilingual settings, their performance remains uneven across languages as LLMs often rely on English-centric latent representations. In this work, we introduce code-switching in-context learning (CSICL), an inference-time mechanism for cross-lingual representational alignment. Rather than relying on an abrupt translation pivot, CSICL explicitly scaffolds the reasoning trajectory by gradually transitioning from a target language to English, aligning non-English inputs with an English-centric reasoning space. Across 4 LLMs, 6 datasets, and 10 languages, CSICL consistently outperforms cross-lingual in-context learning baselines, yielding average gains of 6.0pp and 4.8pp in target and unseen languages, respectively. The improvements generalize across language families and are even more pronounced in low-resource settings, with gains of 14.7pp in target and 5.3pp in unseen languages. These findings establish code-switching as a robust and effective approach for reducing cross-lingual misalignment during inference, moving LLMs towards more equitable and effective multilingual systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"COLM 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05678","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:54:27Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:54:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-14T02:22:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05589","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05589","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05589","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kangjia","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Kangjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenxi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Chenxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Miao","name":"Miao, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinle","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xinle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenjuan","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Chenjuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Invariant Representation Learning for Source-Free Time Series Forecasting with LLM-Centric Proxy Denoising"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T05:29:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:25:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-31T05:24:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-03T01:27:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-06T10:06:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-07T00:39:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Effective time series forecasting enables various real-world applications, benefiting from the proliferation of mobile devices. However, the volume of time series data may vary significantly across domains due to high data acquisition costs and data regulations. To maximally create value from sparse data, this study focuses on a new problem of source-free time series forecasting, aiming to adapt a pretrained model from sufficient source time series to the sparse target time series without access to the source data, enabling data protection. To achieve this, we propose TimeID, a novel source-free time series forecasting framework with a large language model (LLM) centric proxy denoising inspired by the powerful generalization capabilities of LLMs. Specifically, TimeID consists of three key components: (1) dual-branch invariant disentangled feature learning that enforces representation- and gradient-wise invariance by means of season-trend decomposition; (2) lightweight, parameter-free proxy denoising that dynamically calibrates systematic biases of LLMs; and (3) knowledge distillation that bidirectionally aligns the denoised prediction and the original target prediction. Extensive experiments on real-world datasets demonstrate that TimeID outperforms state-of-the-art baselines, improving MSE and MAE by 10.7% and 9.3% on average. The code is available at https://github.com/decisionintelligence/TimeID."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICML2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05589","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:52:23Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:52:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-07T02:15:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05698","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05698","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05698","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yousef","familyName":"Emami","name":"Emami, Yousef","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miguel Gutierrez","familyName":"Gaitan","name":"Gaitan, Miguel Gutierrez","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Atefeh Hajijamali","familyName":"Arani","name":"Arani, Atefeh Hajijamali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingjing","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Jingjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AIC-VDS: Attention-Based In-Context Learning for Joint Velocity Control and Data Collection Scheduling in Multi-UAV-Assisted Pipeline Monitoring"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"C.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"53-01","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T09:04:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:34:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-03T14:54:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-04T02:04:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Uncrewed aerial vehicles (UAVs) are increasingly deployed for autonomous inspection and sensor data collection in large-scale infrastructure monitoring applications, such as pipeline monitoring, where timely anomaly detection is critical. Jointly optimizing data-collection schedules and flight velocities is a critical challenge, as inefficiencies can increase packet loss and inspection latency. While online deep reinforcement learning (DRL) is a widely investigated approach, it suffers from low sample efficiency, substantial training requirements, and simulation-to-reality gaps in time-sensitive scenarios. Large language models (LLMs) offer a promising alternative through in-context learning (ICL); however, their substantial input requirements can introduce considerable computational and communication overhead. To address this, we propose Attention-Based In-Context Learning for Velocity Control and Data Collection Scheduling (AIC-VDS), a joint optimization framework designed to minimize packet loss under partial and potentially outdated local network-state information. AIC-VDS utilizes an attention module to process real-time network-state data, including sensor battery levels, sensor queue lengths, communication channel conditions, UAV locations, time since the previous sensor visit, and sensor urgency scores. This module extracts task-relevant features to reduce input overhead before querying the LLM. The LLM leverages these compressed natural-language prompts to generate adaptive data-collection schedules and velocity-control decisions for UAV execution. Simulation results show that the attention-based representation reduces the average prompt length by 50\\%, while AIC-VDS rapidly stabilizes packet loss in the considered scenario."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05698","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:54:55Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:54:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T04:36:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05554","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05554","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05554","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Shi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengjiang","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Zhengjiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yury","familyName":"Polyanskiy","name":"Polyanskiy, Yury","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philippe","familyName":"Rigollet","name":"Rigollet, Philippe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Critical attention scaling in long-context transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Discrete Mathematics (cs.DM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Classical Analysis and ODEs (math.CA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T03:51:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:22:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-30T05:50:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-31T00:28:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models scale to longer contexts, attention layers suffer from a fundamental pathology: attention scores collapse toward uniformity as context length $n$ increases, causing tokens to cluster excessively, a phenomenon known as rank-collapse. While $\\textit{attention scaling}$ effectively addresses this deficiency by rescaling attention scores with a polylogarithmic factor $β_n$, theoretical justification for this approach remains lacking.\n We analyze a simplified yet tractable model that magnifies the effect of attention scaling. In this model, attention exhibits a phase transition governed by the scaling factor $β_n$: insufficient scaling collapses all tokens to a single direction, while excessive scaling reduces attention to identity, thereby eliminating meaningful interactions between tokens. Our main result identifies the critical scaling $β_n \\asymp \\log n$ and provides a rigorous justification for attention scaling in YaRN and Qwen, clarifying why logarithmic scaling maintains sparse, content-adaptive attention at large context lengths."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"31 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05554","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:51:34Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:51:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-31T02:35:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05888","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05888","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05888","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arefin Ittesafun","familyName":"Abian","name":"Abian, Arefin Ittesafun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Debopom","familyName":"Sutradhar","name":"Sutradhar, Debopom","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Rafi Ur","familyName":"Rashid","name":"Rashid, Md Rafi Ur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reem E.","familyName":"Mohamed","name":"Mohamed, Reem E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Rafiqul","familyName":"Islam","name":"Islam, Md Rafiqul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Asif","familyName":"Karim","name":"Karim, Asif","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kheng Cher","familyName":"Yeo","name":"Yeo, Kheng Cher","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sami","familyName":"Azam","name":"Azam, Sami","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BioAutoML-NAS: An End-to-End AutoML Framework for Multimodal Insect Classification via Neural Architecture Search on Large-Scale Biodiversity Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T13:00:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:46:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T05:56:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-27T00:36:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/tbdata.2026.3705415","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Insect classification is important for agricultural management and ecological research, as it directly affects crop health and production. However, this task remains challenging due to the complex characteristics of insects, class imbalance, and large-scale datasets. To address these issues, we propose BioAutoML-NAS, the first BioAutoML model using multimodal data, including images, and metadata, which applies neural architecture search (NAS) for images to automatically learn the best operations for each connection within each cell. Multiple cells are stacked to form the full network, each extracting detailed image feature representations. A multimodal fusion module combines image embeddings with metadata, allowing the model to use both visual and categorical biological information to classify insects. An alternating bi-level optimization training strategy jointly updates network weights and architecture parameters, while zero operations remove less important connections, producing sparse, efficient, and high-performing architectures. Extensive evaluation on the BIOSCAN-5M dataset demonstrates that BioAutoML-NAS achieves 96.81% accuracy, 97.46% precision, 96.81% recall, and a 97.05% F1 score, outperforming state-of-the-art transfer learning, transformer, AutoML, and NAS methods by approximately 16%, 10%, and 8% respectively. Further validation on the Insects-1M dataset obtains 93.25% accuracy, 93.71% precision, 92.74% recall, and a 93.22% F1 score. These results demonstrate that BioAutoML-NAS provides accurate, confident insect classification that supports modern sustainable farming."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted in IEEE Transactions on Big Data"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05888","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-08T01:59:21Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:59:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-27T01:52:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05592","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05592","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05592","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuofeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhuofeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoxiang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Haoxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seungju","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Seungju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianwen","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Jianwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yejin","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Yejin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Pan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"In-the-Flow Agentic System Optimization for Effective Planning and Tool Use"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T05:32:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:26:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-22T06:43:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-23T00:25:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Outcome-driven reinforcement learning has advanced reasoning in large language models (LLMs), but prevailing tool-augmented approaches train a single, monolithic policy that interleaves thoughts and tool calls under full context; this scales poorly with long horizons and diverse tools and generalizes weakly to new scenarios. Agentic systems offer a promising alternative by decomposing work across specialized modules, yet most remain training-free or rely on offline training decoupled from the live dynamics of multi-turn interaction. We introduce AgentFlow, a trainable, in-the-flow agentic framework that coordinates four modules (planner, executor, verifier, generator) through an evolving memory and directly optimizes its planner inside the multi-turn loop. To train on-policy in live environments, we propose Flow-based Group Refined Policy Optimization (Flow-GRPO), which tackles long-horizon, sparse-reward credit assignment by converting multi-turn optimization into a sequence of tractable single-turn policy updates. It broadcasts a single, verifiable trajectory-level outcome to every turn to align local planner decisions with global success and stabilizes learning with group-normalized advantages. Across ten benchmarks, AgentFlow with a 7B-scale backbone outperforms top-performing baselines with average accuracy gains of 14.9% on search, 14.0% on agentic, 14.5% on mathematical, and 4.1% on scientific tasks, even surpassing larger proprietary models like GPT-4o. Further analyses confirm the benefits of in-the-flow optimization, showing improved planning, enhanced tool-calling reliability, and positive scaling with model size and reasoning turns."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"47 pages, 12 figures. ICLR 2026 Oral. Project website: https://agentflow.stanford.edu/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05592","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:52:27Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:52:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-23T02:07:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05743","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05743","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05743","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Petter","familyName":"Holme","name":"Holme, Petter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Milena","familyName":"Tsvetkova","name":"Tsvetkova, Milena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Artificially intelligent agents in the social and behavioral sciences: A history and outlook"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T10:02:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:38:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-31T08:48:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-03T01:33:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-19T04:13:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-21T00:48:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We review the historical development and current trends of artificially intelligent agents (agentic AI) in the social and behavioral sciences: from the first programmable computers, and social simulations soon thereafter, to today's experiments with large language models. This overview emphasizes the role of AI in the scientific process and the changes brought about, both through technological advancements and the broader evolution of science from around 1950 to the present. Some of the specific points we cover include: the challenges of presenting the first social simulation studies to a world unaware of computers, the rise of social systems science, intelligent game theoretic agents, the age of big data and the epistemic upheaval in its wake, and the current enthusiasm around applications of generative AI, and many other topics. A pervasive theme is how deeply entwined we are with the technologies we use to understand ourselves."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05743","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:55:58Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:55:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-21T03:43:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05969","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05969","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05969","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sunbowen","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Sunbowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingyu","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Qingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chak Tou","familyName":"Leong","name":"Leong, Chak Tou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialiang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jialiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yicheng","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Yicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiwen","familyName":"Ni","name":"Ni, Shiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyu","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Xiaoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Probing the Difficulty Perception Mechanism of Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T14:24:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:51:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-12T07:13:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:55:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-19T06:24:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-07-21T00:50:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed on complex reasoning tasks, yet little is known about their ability to internally evaluate problem difficulty, which is an essential capability for adaptive reasoning and efficient resource allocation. In this work, we investigate whether LLMs implicitly encode problem difficulty in their internal representations. Using a linear probe on the final-token representations of LLMs, we demonstrate that the difficulty level of math problems can be linearly modeled. We further locate the specific attention heads of the final Transformer layer: these attention heads have opposite activation patterns for simple and difficult problems, thus achieving perception of difficulty. Our ablation experiments prove the accuracy of the location. Crucially, our experiments provide practical support for using LLMs as automatic difficulty annotators, potentially substantially reducing reliance on costly human labeling in benchmark construction and curriculum learning. We also uncover that there is a significant difference in entropy and difficulty perception at the token level. Our study reveals that difficulty perception in LLMs is not only present but also structurally organized, offering new theoretical insights and practical directions for future research. Our code is available at https://github.com/Aegis1863/Difficulty-Perception-of-LLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05969","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:01:18Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:01:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-21T03:43:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05860","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05860","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05860","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luka","familyName":"Nenadic","name":"Nenadic, Luka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Rodriguez","name":"Rodriguez, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joseph A.","familyName":"Calandrino","name":"Calandrino, Joseph A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Overcoming Language Barriers: Multilingual Analysis of the 2023 Swiss Privacy Law's Impact"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T12:30:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:44:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-15T05:56:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-16T00:24:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.56553/popets-2026-0141","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Policymakers enact and revise privacy laws expecting meaningful benefits for their people in practice. While scholarship has measured the real-world impact of some privacy regulations-the EU and California most notably-limited empirical evidence exists for many of the more than 140 countries that have implemented some form of privacy legislation. Switzerland, a multilingual country bordered almost entirely by EU states, is one such example.\n This paper analyzes the extent to which a 2023 alignment of Swiss privacy law with EU privacy regulation affected website privacy policies in Switzerland. To address Switzerland's unique multilingual culture, we develop an LLM-based pipeline that extracts legally relevant information as document-level labels in a single inference without requiring translation. On a benchmark of 120 expert-annotated privacy policies in German, French, Italian, and English, our pipeline achieves F1 scores above 0.90 for most pairs of languages and legally relevant disclosures.\n Applying this pipeline to privacy policies we collected from more than 35,000 Swiss- and EU-facing websites before and after the 2023 privacy law revision, we find significant increases in both mandatory and voluntary disclosures of data subject rights among Swiss privacy policies. In exploring the mechanisms driving increased disclosure rates, we discover heavy use of automated privacy policy generators and find that generated policies are associated with up to 15 percentage points higher disclosure rates. These results provide large-scale empirical evidence of how regulatory change and novel drafting technologies impact the content of privacy policies in a unique multilingual environment."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05860","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-08T01:58:43Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:58:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-16T02:34:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05681","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05681","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05681","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suhyeok","familyName":"Jang","name":"Jang, Suhyeok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongyoung","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Dongyoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changyeon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Changyeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youngsuk","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Youngsuk","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinwoo","familyName":"Shin","name":"Shin, Jinwoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Verifier-free Test-Time Sampling for Vision-Language-Action Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T08:38:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:33:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-04T15:45:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-07T01:19:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language-Action models (VLAs) have demonstrated remarkable performance in robot control. However, they remain fundamentally limited in tasks that require high precision due to their single-inference paradigm. While test-time scaling approaches using external verifiers have shown promise, they require additional training and fail to generalize to unseen conditions. We propose Masking Distribution Guided Selection (MG-Select), a novel test-time scaling framework for VLAs that leverages the model's internal properties without requiring additional training or external modules. Our approach utilizes KL divergence from a reference action token distribution as a confidence metric for selecting the optimal action from multiple candidates. We introduce a reference distribution generated by the same VLA but with randomly masked states and language conditions as inputs, providing action uncertainty while remaining aligned with the target task distribution. Additionally, we propose a joint training strategy that enables the model to learn both conditional and unconditional distributions by applying dropout to state and language conditions, thereby further improving the quality of the reference distribution. Our experiments demonstrate that MG-Select provides a reliable reference for action selection through task-relevant condition masking and consistently improves base models across diverse simulation and real-world benchmarks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICLR 2026. Project page: https://suhyeok-jang.github.io/mg-select/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05681","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:54:31Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:54:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-07T04:58:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06096","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06096","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06096","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthieu","familyName":"Bou","name":"Bou, Matthieu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nyal","familyName":"Patel","name":"Patel, Nyal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arjun","familyName":"Jagota","name":"Jagota, Arjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satyapriya","familyName":"Krishna","name":"Krishna, Satyapriya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sonali","familyName":"Parbhoo","name":"Parbhoo, Sonali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Alignment Auditor: A Bayesian Framework for Verifying and Refining LLM Objectives"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T16:25:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:58:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T10:07:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:38:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-26T18:48:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-30T00:07:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The objectives that Large Language Models (LLMs) implicitly optimize remain dangerously opaque, making trustworthy alignment and auditing a grand challenge. While Inverse Reinforcement Learning (IRL) can infer reward functions from behaviour, existing approaches either produce a single, overconfident reward estimate or fail to address the fundamental ambiguity of the task (non-identifiability). This paper introduces a principled auditing framework that re-frames reward inference from a simple estimation task to a comprehensive process for verification. Our framework leverages Bayesian IRL to not only recover a distribution over objectives but to enable three critical audit capabilities: (i) Quantifying and systematically reducing non-identifiability by demonstrating posterior contraction over sequential rounds of evidence; (ii) Providing actionable, uncertainty-aware diagnostics that expose spurious shortcuts and identify out-of-distribution prompts where the inferred objective cannot be trusted; and (iii) Validating policy-level utility by showing that the refined, low-uncertainty reward can be used directly in RLHF to achieve training dynamics and toxicity reductions comparable to the ground-truth alignment process. Empirically, our framework successfully audits a detoxified LLM, yielding a well-calibrated and interpretable objective that strengthens alignment guarantees. Overall, this work provides a practical toolkit for auditors, safety teams, and regulators to verify what LLMs are truly trying to achieve, moving us toward more trustworthy and accountable AI."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06096","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:04:17Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:04:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-30T04:07:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04961","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04961","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04961","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Théophane","familyName":"Vallaeys","name":"Vallaeys, Théophane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jakob","familyName":"Verbeek","name":"Verbeek, Jakob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthieu","familyName":"Cord","name":"Cord, Matthieu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SSDD: Single-Step Diffusion Decoder for Efficient Image Tokenization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T15:57:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:42:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-29T15:33:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-30T02:07:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Tokenizers are a key component of state-of-the-art generative image models, extracting the most important features from the signal while reducing data dimension and redundancy. Most current tokenizers are based on KL-regularized variational autoencoders (KL-VAE), trained with reconstruction, perceptual and adversarial losses. Diffusion decoders have been proposed as a more principled alternative to model the distribution over images conditioned on the latent. However, matching the performance of KL-VAE still requires adversarial losses, as well as a higher decoding time due to iterative sampling. To address these limitations, we introduce a new pixel diffusion decoder architecture for improved scaling and training stability, benefiting from transformer components and GAN-free training. We use distillation to replicate the performance of the diffusion decoder in an efficient single-step decoder. This makes SSDD the first diffusion decoder optimized for single-step reconstruction trained without adversarial losses, reaching higher reconstruction quality and faster sampling than KL-VAE. In particular, SSDD improves reconstruction FID from $0.87$ to $0.46$ with $1.4\\times$ higher throughput and preserve generation quality of DiTs with $3.8\\times$ faster sampling. As such, SSDD can be used as a drop-in replacement for KL-VAE, and for building higher-quality and faster generative models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04961","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:00:29Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:00:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-30T04:07:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05174","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05174","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05174","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christoph","familyName":"Riedl","name":"Riedl, Christoph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Emergent Coordination in Multi-Agent Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2; I.2.11","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T11:26:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T21:22:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T01:12:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-15T14:32:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-17T01:22:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-28T21:26:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06-24T00:54:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"When are multi-agent LLM systems merely a collection of individual agents versus an integrated collective with higher-order structure? We introduce an information-theoretic framework to test -- in a purely data-driven way -- whether multi-agent systems show signs of higher-order structure. This information decomposition lets us measure whether dynamical emergence is present in multi-agent LLM systems, localize it, and distinguish spurious temporal coupling from performance-relevant cross-agent synergy. We implement a practical criterion and an emergence capacity criterion operationalized as partial information decomposition of time-delayed mutual information (TDMI). We apply our framework to experiments using a simple guessing game without direct agent communication and minimal group-level feedback with three randomized interventions. Groups in the control condition exhibit strong temporal synergy but little coordinated alignment across agents. Assigning a persona to each agent introduces stable identity-linked differentiation. Combining personas with an instruction to ``think about what other agents might do'' shows identity-linked differentiation and goal-directed complementarity across agents. Taken together, our framework establishes that multi-agent LLM systems can be steered with prompt design from mere aggregates to higher-order collectives. Our results are robust across emergence measures and entropy estimators, and not explained by coordination-free baselines or temporal dynamics alone. Without attributing human-like cognition to the agents, the patterns of interaction we observe mirror well-established principles of collective intelligence in human groups: effective performance requires both alignment on shared objectives and complementary contributions across members."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05174","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":2,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:42:47Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:42:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-24T02:27:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05126","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05126","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05126","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mark","familyName":"Steyvers","name":"Steyvers, Mark","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Catarina","familyName":"Belem","name":"Belem, Catarina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Padhraic","familyName":"Smyth","name":"Smyth, Padhraic","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generalization of Fine-Tuned Uncertainty Communication and Metacognition in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T19:50:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:00:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-21T21:46:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-23T00:10:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-18T21:02:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-23T00:14:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Background. Large language models are increasingly used in settings where confident but incorrect answers can mislead users. Reliable uncertainty communication requires a form of metacognition: monitoring when one's own answers are likely to be correct. Yet models' stated confidence is often poorly aligned with answer correctness. We test whether supervised fine-tuning improves uncertainty communication and whether gains transfer across domains and task formats.\n Methods. We fine-tuned two models on general knowledge, mathematics, and open-ended trivia questions. We evaluated single-question confidence estimation, in which the model reports numeric confidence for one answer, and pairwise confidence comparison, in which it chooses which of two questions it is more likely to answer correctly. We tested held-out questions from training domains and new medical, legal, and truthfulness benchmarks. We assessed calibration, discrimination, and answer accuracy before and after fine-tuning.\n Results. Here we show that fine-tuning improves alignment between stated confidence and observed accuracy and increases the model's ability to assign higher confidence to correct than to incorrect answers. Gains occur within training domains and, to a lesser extent, in new domains. However, single-task training does not reliably transfer between single-question confidence estimation and pairwise confidence comparison. Multitask fine-tuning produces broader gains in the models and tasks studied here.\n Conclusions. Uncertainty communication in large language models is trainable, but transfer across metacognitive tasks is limited. Joint training on multiple confidence tasks may support broader generalization, although further tests across model families and metacognitive tasks are needed."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05126","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:41:41Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:41:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-23T05:34:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06048","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06048","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06048","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Hao","name":"Hao, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huixia","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Huixia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingrui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Mingrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BLISS: A Lightweight Bilevel Influence Scoring Method for Data Selection in Language Model Pretraining"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:42:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:55:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T17:49:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:03:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-02T22:11:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-04T01:13:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-31T22:01:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06-02T01:25:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06-18T04:28:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-06-19T00:23:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Effective data selection is essential for pretraining large language models (LLMs), enhancing efficiency and improving generalization to downstream tasks. However, existing approaches often require leveraging external pretrained models, making it difficult to disentangle the effects of data selection from those of the external pretrained models. In addition, they often overlook the long-term impact of selected data if the model is trained to convergence, primarily due to the prohibitive cost of full-scale LLM pretraining. In this paper, we introduce BLISS (\\textbf{B}ileve\\textbf{L} \\textbf{I}nfluence \\textbf{S}coring method for data \\textbf{S}election): a lightweight data selection method that operates entirely \\emph{from scratch}, without relying on any external pretrained oracle models, while explicitly accounting for the long-term impact of selected data. BLISS leverages a small proxy model as a surrogate for the LLM and employs a score model to estimate the long-term influence of training samples if the proxy model is trained to convergence. We formulate data selection as a bilevel optimization problem, where the upper-level objective optimizes the score model to assign importance weights to training samples, ensuring that minimizing the lower-level objective (i.e., training the proxy model over the weighted training loss until convergence) leads to best validation performance. Once optimized, the trained score model predicts influence scores for the dataset, enabling efficient selection of high-quality samples for LLM pretraining. We validate BLISS by pretraining 410M/1B/2.8B Pythia and LLaMA-0.5B models on selected subsets of the C4 dataset. Notably, under the 1B model setting, BLISS achieves $1.7\\times$ speedup in reaching the same performance as the state-of-the-art method, demonstrating superior performance across multiple downstream tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06048","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:03:10Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:03:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-19T02:17:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05013","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05013","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05013","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Theodore Jerome","familyName":"Tinker","name":"Tinker, Theodore Jerome","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kenji","familyName":"Doya","name":"Doya, Kenji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Tani","name":"Tani, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Curiosity-Driven Development of Action and Language in Robots Through Self-Exploration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T16:53:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:45:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-23T02:20:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-24T00:18:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-02T03:27:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-03T01:21:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-04T01:36:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-12-05T01:12:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-12-16T05:35:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-12-17T01:24:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-06-03T07:36:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-06-04T00:34:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-06-18T04:26:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v7"},{"date":"2026-06-19T00:23:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v7"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"7","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Infants acquire language with generalization from minimal experience, whereas large language models require billions of training tokens. What underlies efficient development in humans? We investigated this problem through experiments wherein robotic agents learn to perform actions associated with imperative sentences (e.g., push red cube) via curiosity-driven self-exploration. Our approach amortizes active inference using Q-learning, enabling intrinsically motivated developmental learning. The simulations reveal key findings corresponding to observations in developmental psychology. i) Generalization improves drastically as the scale of compositional elements increases. ii) Curiosity-driven exploration enables faster learning. iii) Rote pairing of sentences and actions precedes compositional generalization. iv) Exception-handling induces U-shaped developmental performance, a pattern like representational redescription in child language learning. These results suggest that curiosity-driven active inference accounts for how intrinsically motivated sensorimotor-linguistic learning supports scalable compositional generalization and exception handling in humans and artificial agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 22 pages of supplementary material"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05013","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":6,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:02:40Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:02:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-19T02:17:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05107","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05107","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05107","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Myung Ho","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Myung Ho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Structured Cognitive Loop for Behavioral Intelligence in Large Language Model Agents (Extended Revision: From Behavioral Architecture to Epistemic Accountability)"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-23T17:43:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:00:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-04T05:15:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-05T01:23:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-12T08:18:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-13T01:31:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-28T14:49:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-12-01T02:31:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-06-17T14:35:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-06-18T00:57:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The central challenge for AI agents is not only performance but accountability. Agents that act through opaque prompt sequences may produce correct outputs, but they provide little basis for verifying why an action was permitted, where an error occurred, or how responsibility should be assigned. This paper presents the Structured Cognitive Loop as an architecture for accountable behavior in large language model agents. SCL separates cognition, memory, control, and action into distinct modules. The language model proposes. External memory preserves verified state. A lightweight controller checks preconditions, prevents redundant actions, and authorizes execution before tools are used. We evaluate SCL against ReAct and common LangChain agent variants across travel planning, conditional email drafting, and constraint guided image generation. Across 360 episodes, SCL achieves 86.3 percent task success compared with 70.5 to 76.8 percent for prompt based baselines. It also improves goal fidelity, reduces redundant tool calls, increases reuse of intermediate state, and lowers unsupported assertions. This extended revision situates SCL within a broader architecture of epistemic accountability. Subsequent extensions integrate context aware Human in the Loop control, Pool Gated Retrieval, and the Horizon Warrant Commitment framework. Together these components define an agent architecture in which the model proposes, structure decides, evidence is warranted before use, and human judgment is embedded in the trace rather than imposed after the fact. The result is a foundation for AI agents whose decisions are not only effective but also authorized, inspectable, and accountable."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This revised version extends the original SCL framework from a behavioral architecture for reliable LLM agents into a broader architecture of epistemic accountability, integrating context-aware Human-in-the-Loop control, Pool-Gated Retrieval, and the Horizon-Warrant-Commitment structure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05107","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:41:15Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:41:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-18T02:18:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06143","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06143","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06143","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan","familyName":"Cegin","name":"Cegin, Jan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Branislav","familyName":"Pecher","name":"Pecher, Branislav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ivan","familyName":"Srba","name":"Srba, Ivan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jakub","familyName":"Simko","name":"Simko, Jakub","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RoSE: Round-robin Synthetic Data Evaluation for Selecting LLM Generators without Human Test Sets"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:17:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:00:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T12:16:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-16T01:42:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLMs are powerful generators of synthetic data, which are used for training smaller, specific models. This is especially valuable for low-resource languages, where human-labelled data is scarce but LLMs can still produce high-quality text. However, LLMs differ in how useful their outputs are for training. Selecting the best LLM as a generator is challenging because extrinsic evaluation requires costly human annotations (which are often unavailable for low-resource languages), while intrinsic metrics correlate poorly with downstream performance. We introduce Round robin Synthetic data Evaluation (RoSE), a proxy metric for selecting the best LLM generator without human test sets. RoSE trains a small model on the outputs of a candidate generator (LLM) and then evaluates it on generated synthetic examples from all other candidate LLMs. The final RoSE score is the mean performance of this small model. Across six LLMs, eleven languages, and three tasks (sentiment, topic, intent), RoSE identifies the optimal generator more often than any other intrinsic heuristics. RoSE outperforms intrinsic heuristics and comes within 0.76 percentage points of the optimal generator baseline. This result is measured in terms of downstream performance, obtained by training a small model on the chosen generator's outputs (optimal vs. proxy metric selected) and evaluating it on human-labelled test data. Additionally, RoSE is the only metric to achieve a positive correlation with performance on human test data."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages; EACL 2026 Main"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06143","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:05:22Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:05:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-16T04:06:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05150","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05150","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05150","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donghang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Donghang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Haoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuerui","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xuerui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gang","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hexin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hexin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nana","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Nana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchen","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eng Siong","familyName":"Chng","name":"Chng, Eng Siong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Chronological Thinking in Full-Duplex Spoken Dialogue Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T10:28:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T21:35:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:11:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-12T14:26:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:51:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in spoken dialogue language models (SDLMs) reflect growing interest in shifting from turn-based to full-duplex systems, where the models continuously perceive user speech streams while generating responses. This simultaneous listening and speaking design enables real-time interaction and the agent can handle dynamic conversational behaviors like user barge-in. However, during the listening phase, existing systems keep the agent idle by repeatedly predicting the silence token, which departs from human behavior: we usually engage in lightweight thinking during conversation rather than remaining absent-minded. Inspired by this, we propose Chronological Thinking, an on-the-fly conversational thinking mechanism that aims to improve response quality in full-duplex SDLMs. Specifically, chronological thinking presents a paradigm shift from conventional LLM thinking approaches, such as Chain-of-Thought, purpose-built for streaming acoustic input. (1) Strictly causal: the agent reasons incrementally while listening, updating internal hypotheses only from past audio with no lookahead. (2) No additional latency: reasoning is amortized during the listening window; once the user stops speaking, the agent halts thinking and begins speaking without further delay. Experiments demonstrate the effectiveness of chronological thinking through both objective metrics and human evaluations show consistent improvements in response quality. Furthermore, chronological thinking robustly handles conversational dynamics and attains competitive performance on full-duplex interaction metrics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by SIGDIAL 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05150","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:42:14Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:42:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-15T02:03:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05430","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05430","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05430","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huayi","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Huayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pratik","familyName":"Chaudhari","name":"Chaudhari, Pratik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Active Semantic Perception"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T22:49:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:14:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-11T00:24:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-12T00:16:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We develop an approach for active semantic perception, which refers to using the semantics of the scene for tasks such as exploration. We build a compact, multi-layer scene graph that can represent large, complex indoor environments at various levels of abstraction, e.g., nodes corresponding to rooms, objects, walls, windows etc., as well as fine-grained details of their geometry. We develop a procedure based on large language models (LLMs) to sample new plausible scene graphs of unobserved regions that are consistent with partial observations of the scene. We develop a procedure to compute the information gain of a potential waypoint upon this scene graph to enable sophisticated spatial reasoning: for example, of the two doors that lead out of the living room, one probably leads to the kitchen and the other to the bedroom. We evaluate our approach in realistic 3D indoor apartments in simulation and also on a Unitree Go 2 robot in the real world. Qualitative and quantitative analysis shows that our approach can pin down high-level and low-level semantic information in the environment quickly and more accurately than existing approaches."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05430","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:48:43Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:48:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-12T02:19:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05478","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05478","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05478","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Haoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxian","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Jiaxian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yusuke","familyName":"Iwasawa","name":"Iwasawa, Yusuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yutaka","familyName":"Matsuo","name":"Matsuo, Yutaka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AQA-TTRL: Self-Adaptation in Audio Question Answering with Test-Time Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T00:39:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:16:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-22T10:18:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-23T01:33:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-08T05:16:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-09T01:22:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Audio Language Models (LALMs) exhibit strong capabilities in general audio understanding but remain static after deployment, limiting their adaptability to real-world data. Since supervised fine-tuning is costly, we propose AQA-TTRL, a novel framework for audio understanding that enables on-the-fly evolution via test-time reinforcement learning using only unlabeled test data. It generates pseudo-labels via majority voting and optimizes the model through reinforcement learning. To address the noise in self-generated labels, we introduce confidence weighting to adjust training signals. Furthermore, multiple-attempt sampling mitigates advantage collapse and stabilizes training. Across MMAU, MMAR, and MMSU, AQA-TTRL achieves significant average improvements of 4.42% for Qwen2.5-Omni 7B and 11.04% for the 3B model. Notably, the adapted 3B model outperforms direct inference of the unadapted 7B model, highlighting the effectiveness of test-time adaptation in audio understanding."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to INTERSPEECH 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05478","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:49:49Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:49:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-09T04:13:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05255","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05255","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05255","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Farhad","familyName":"Rezazadeh","name":"Rezazadeh, Farhad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hatim","familyName":"Chergui","name":"Chergui, Hatim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Merouane","familyName":"Debbah","name":"Debbah, Merouane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Houbing","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Houbing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingjia","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Lingjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rivaling Transformers: Multi-Scale Structured State-Space Mixtures for Agentic 6G O-RAN"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T18:17:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:04:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-08T07:39:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-09T01:27:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In sixth-generation (6G) Open Radio Access Networks (O-RAN), proactive control is preferable. A key open challenge is delivering control-grade predictions within Near-Real-Time (Near-RT) latency and computational constraints under multi-timescale dynamics. We therefore cast RAN Intelligent Controller (RIC) analytics as an agentic perceive-predict xApp that turns noisy, multivariate RAN telemetry into short-horizon per-User Equipment (UE) key performance indicator (KPI) forecasts to drive anticipatory control. In this regard, Transformers are powerful for sequence learning and time-series forecasting, but they are memory-intensive, which limits Near-RT RIC use. Therefore, we need models that maintain accuracy while reducing latency and data movement. To this end, we propose a lightweight Multi-Scale Structured State-Space Mixtures (MS3M) forecaster that mixes HiPPO-LegS kernels to capture multi-timescale radio dynamics. We develop stable discrete state-space models (SSMs) via bilinear (Tustin) discretization and apply their causal impulse responses as per-feature depthwise convolutions. Squeeze-and-Excitation gating dynamically reweights KPI channels as conditions change, and a compact gated channel-mixing layer models cross-feature nonlinearities without Transformer-level cost. The model is KPI-agnostic -- Reference Signal Received Power (RSRP) serves as a canonical use case -- and is trained on sliding windows to predict the immediate next step. Empirical evaluations conducted using our bespoke O-RAN testbed KPI time-series dataset (59,441 windows across 13 KPIs). Crucially for O-RAN constraints, MS3M achieves a 0.057 s per-inference latency with 0.70M parameters, yielding 3-10x lower latency than the Transformer baselines evaluated on the same hardware, while maintaining competitive accuracy."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 2 Figures, 5 Tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05255","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:44:41Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:44:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-09T04:13:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05363","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05363","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05363","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhinav","familyName":"Jain","name":"Jain, Abhinav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Reps","name":"Reps, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher","familyName":"Jermaine","name":"Jermaine, Christopher","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MHA-RAG: Improving Efficiency, Accuracy, and Consistency by Encoding Exemplars as Soft Prompts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T20:41:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:10:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-04T21:50:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-08T00:10:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Adapting Foundation Models to new domains with limited training data is challenging and computationally expensive. While prior work has demonstrated the effectiveness of using domain-specific exemplars as in-context demonstrations, we investigate whether representing exemplars purely as text is the most efficient, effective, and stable approach. We explore an alternative: representing exemplars as soft prompts with an exemplar order invariant model architecture. To this end, we introduce Multi-Head Attention Retrieval-Augmented Generation (MHA-RAG), a framework with the number of attention heads serving as a simple hyperparameter to control soft prompt-generation across different tasks. Across multiple question-answering benchmarks and model scales, MHA-RAG achieves a 20-point performance gain over standard RAG, while cutting inference costs by a factor of 10X GFLOPs-delivering both higher accuracy and greater efficiency, invariant to exemplar order."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05363","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:47:12Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:47:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-08T02:01:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05709","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05709","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05709","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mary","familyName":"Llewellyn","name":"Llewellyn, Mary","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Isobel","familyName":"Thornton","name":"Thornton, Isobel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Bishop","name":"Bishop, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Annie","familyName":"Gray","name":"Gray, Annie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Correcting Prompt Dependence in LLM Benchmarks: A Bayesian Hierarchical Model with Embedding-Space Clustering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T09:22:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:35:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-04T12:15:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-05T00:54:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLM benchmarking metrics often misstate performance and uncertainty as they rely on two assumptions that frequently do not hold in practice: (i) a sufficient number of evaluations are available for classical inference, and (ii) test prompts are independent. We propose a corrective Bayesian hierarchical model with embedding-space clustering that provides robust performance metrics in limited-data settings while correcting for prompt dependence. We apply the approach to adversarial robustness benchmarks, showing consistent recovery of clustering structure, resulting in more reliable performance metrics, with 4-73% improvements to mean absolute errors and 40-450 unit improvements to expected log posterior densities."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to the 1st Workshop on Combining Theory and Benchmarks, CTB@ICML 2026, Seoul, South Korea"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05709","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:55:11Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:55:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-05T07:04:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05566","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05566","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05566","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhexiao","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Zhexiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanyuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yuanyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Neeraj","familyName":"Sarna","name":"Sarna, Neeraj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanyuan","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Yuanyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"von Gablenz","name":"von Gablenz, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Domain-Shift-Aware Conformal Prediction for Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Applications (stat.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T04:22:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:23:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-31T19:40:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-02T01:22:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models have achieved impressive performance across diverse tasks. However, their tendency to produce overconfident and factually incorrect outputs, known as hallucinations, poses risks in real-world applications. Conformal prediction provides finite-sample, distribution-free coverage guarantees, but standard conformal prediction breaks down under domain shift, often leading to under-coverage and unreliable prediction sets. We propose a new framework called Domain-Shift-Aware Conformal Prediction (DS-CP). Our framework adapts conformal prediction to large language models under domain shift, by systematically reweighting calibration samples based on their proximity to the test prompt, thereby preserving validity while enhancing adaptivity. Our theoretical analysis and experiments on the MMLU benchmark demonstrate that the proposed method delivers more reliable coverage than standard conformal prediction, especially under substantial distribution shifts, while maintaining efficiency. This provides a practical step toward trustworthy uncertainty quantification for large language models in real-world deployment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to Forty-Third International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML), 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05566","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:51:51Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:51:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-02T04:53:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05342","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05342","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05342","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyung Gyu","familyName":"Rho","name":"Rho, Hyung Gyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Margin Adaptive DPO: Leveraging Reward Model for Granular Control in Preference Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T20:09:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:08:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-01T10:47:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-02T01:58:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) has emerged as a simple and effective method for aligning large language models. However, its reliance on a fixed temperature parameter leads to suboptimal training on diverse preference data, causing overfitting on easy examples and under-learning from informative ones. Recent methods have emerged to counter this. While IPO addresses general overfitting, its uniform regularization can be overly conservative. The more targeted approach of $β$-DPO suffers from its own limitations: its batch-level adaptation applies a single, compromised temperature to mixed-margin pairs, its linear update rule can produce unstable negative $β$ values, and its filtering mechanism discards potentially useful training signals. In this work, we introduce Margin-Adaptive Direct Preference Optimization (MADPO), a method that provides a stable, data-preserving, and instance-level solution. MADPO employs a practical two-step approach: it first trains a reward model to estimate preference margins and then uses these margins to apply a continuous, adaptive weight to the DPO loss for each individual training sample. This re-weighting scheme creates an effective target margin that is amplified for hard pairs and dampened for easy pairs, allowing for granular control over the learning signal. We provide a comprehensive theoretical analysis, proving that MADPO has a well-behaved optimization landscape and is robust to reward model estimation errors. We validate our theory with experiments on a summarization task using human preference data. MADPO consistently outperforms strong baselines across a comprehensive sweep of decoding temperatures."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05342","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:46:43Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:46:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-02T04:53:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05115","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05115","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05115","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yansen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yansen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingcan","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Qingcan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujie","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yujie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yufei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yufei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiongwei","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Xiongwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingxuan","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Mingxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SAC-Opt: Semantic Anchors for Iterative Correction in Optimization Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-28T12:25:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:00:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-27T03:30:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-28T01:23:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-29T07:07:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-01T00:36:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have opened new paradigms in optimization modeling by enabling the generation of executable solver code from natural language descriptions. Despite this promise, existing approaches typically remain solver-driven: they rely on single-pass forward generation and apply limited post-hoc fixes based on solver error messages, leaving undetected semantic errors that silently produce syntactically correct but logically flawed models. To address this challenge, we propose SAC-Opt, a backward-guided correction framework that grounds optimization modeling in problem semantics rather than solver feedback. At each step, SAC-Opt aligns the original semantic anchors with those reconstructed from the generated code and selectively corrects only the mismatched components, driving convergence toward a semantically faithful model. This anchor-driven correction enables fine-grained refinement of constraint and objective logic, enhancing both fidelity and robustness without requiring additional training or supervision. Empirical results on seven public datasets demonstrate that SAC-Opt improves average modeling accuracy by 7.7%, with gains of up to 21.9% on the ComplexLP dataset. These findings highlight the importance of semantic-anchored correction in LLM-based optimization workflows to ensure faithful translation from problem intent to solver-executable code."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICML 2026 accepted"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05115","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:41:26Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:41:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-01T02:33:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06182","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06182","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06182","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoav","familyName":"Gur-Arieh","name":"Gur-Arieh, Yoav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mor","familyName":"Geva","name":"Geva, Mor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Atticus","familyName":"Geiger","name":"Geiger, Atticus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mixing Mechanisms: How Language Models Retrieve Bound Entities In-Context"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:44:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:02:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-28T05:00:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-29T00:31:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A key component of in-context reasoning is the ability of language models (LMs) to bind entities for later retrieval. For example, an LM might represent \"Ann loves pie\" by binding \"Ann\" to \"pie\", allowing it to later retrieve \"Ann\" when asked \"Who loves pie?\" Prior research on short lists of bound entities found strong evidence that LMs implement such retrieval via a positional mechanism, where \"Ann\" is retrieved based on its position in context. In this work, we find that this mechanism generalizes poorly to more complex settings; as the number of bound entities in context increases, the positional mechanism becomes noisy and unreliable in middle positions. To compensate for this, we find that LMs supplement the positional mechanism with a lexical mechanism (retrieving \"Ann\" using its bound counterpart \"pie\") and a reflexive mechanism (retrieving \"Ann\" through a direct pointer). Through extensive experiments on nine models and ten binding tasks, we uncover a consistent pattern in how LMs mix these mechanisms to drive model behavior. We leverage these insights to develop a causal model combining all three mechanisms that estimates next token distributions with 95% agreement. Finally, we show that our model generalizes to substantially longer inputs of open-ended text interleaved with entity groups, further demonstrating the robustness of our findings in more natural settings. Overall, our study establishes a more complete picture of how LMs bind and retrieve entities in-context."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICLR 2026 Main Conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06182","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:06:17Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:06:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-29T02:57:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06063","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06063","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06063","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Austin","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Austin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andreas","familyName":"Varvarigos","name":"Varvarigos, Andreas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ioannis","familyName":"Panitsas","name":"Panitsas, Ioannis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniela","familyName":"Fernandez","name":"Fernandez, Daniela","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinbiao","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Jinbiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuwei","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Yuwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jialin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Maatouk","name":"Maatouk, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leandros","familyName":"Tassiulas","name":"Tassiulas, Leandros","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rex","familyName":"Ying","name":"Ying, Rex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TelecomTS: A Multi-Modal Observability Dataset for Time Series and Language Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:54:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:56:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-19T22:44:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-21T00:16:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-27T22:16:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-29T00:14:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modern enterprises generate vast streams of time series metrics when monitoring complex systems, known as observability data. Unlike conventional time series from domains such as climate, observability data are zero-inflated, highly stochastic, and exhibit minimal temporal structure. Despite their importance, observability datasets remain underrepresented in public benchmarks due to proprietary restrictions and privacy concerns. Existing datasets are often anonymized and normalized, removing scale information and limiting their use for tasks such as anomaly detection, root cause analysis, and multi-modal reasoning. To address this gap, we introduce TelecomTS, a large-scale observability dataset derived from a 5G telecommunications network. TelecomTS features heterogeneous, de-anonymized covariates with explicit absolute scale information and provides a diverse suite of downstream tasks, including anomaly detection, root cause analysis, and multi-modal question-answering. Benchmarking state-of-the-art time series, language, reasoning, and multi-modal foundation models reveals that existing approaches struggle with the abrupt, noisy, and high-variance dynamics characteristic of observability data. Our experiments further underscore the importance of preserving covariates' absolute scale, emphasizing the need for foundation time series models that natively leverage scale information for practical real-world observability applications. The code is available at: https://github.com/Ali-maatouk/TelecomTS."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06063","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:03:31Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:03:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-29T02:57:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06133","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06133","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06133","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kangyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Kangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyun","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Zhiyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haibo","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Haibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weijia","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Weijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianguo","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jianguo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenzhong","familyName":"Lan","name":"Lan, Zhenzhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiyao","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Weiyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CreditDecoding: Accelerating Parallel Decoding in Diffusion Large Language Models with Trace Credit"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:08:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:00:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-19T15:25:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-21T01:16:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-26T03:39:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-27T00:27:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion large language models (dLLMs) generate text through iterative denoising. In commonly adopted parallel decoding schemes, each step confirms only high-confidence positions while remasking the others. By analyzing dLLM denoising traces, we uncover a key inefficiency: models often predict the correct target token several steps before its confidence becomes high enough to be decoded. This gap between early prediction and late decoding forces repeated remasking of already-correct tokens, causing redundant iterations and limiting acceleration. To exploit this temporal redundancy, we introduce Trace Credit to quantify a token's decoding potential by accumulating historical evidence. Building on this, we propose CreditDecoding, a training-free parallel decoding method that fuses Trace Credit with current logits to boost the confidence of correct but underconfident tokens, thereby accelerating denoising and improving robustness. On eight benchmarks, CreditDecoding achieves up to 5.48 times speedup with +0.48 accuracy on LLaDA-8B and consistently improves performance across diverse dLLM architectures and parameter scales. It further scales to long contexts and remains orthogonal to mainstream inference optimizations, making it a practical and widely applicable solution."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages, 13 figures, 9 tables, Accepted to ACL 2026 main conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06133","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:05:07Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:05:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-27T02:36:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05864","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05864","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05864","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Faeze","familyName":"Ghorbanpour","name":"Ghorbanpour, Faeze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Fraser","name":"Fraser, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the Sensitivity of Instruction-tuned LLMs to Harmful Sentences in Long Inputs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T12:33:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:44:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-26T13:54:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-27T01:02:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) increasingly operate on long inputs, yet their behavior when harmful sentences are sparsely embedded within such inputs remains poorly understood. We present a sensitivity analysis that probes how LLMs extract harmful sentences embedded in long inputs. We construct long inputs by combining neutral and harmful sentences, and systematically vary four factors: input length (600--30,000 tokens), the proportion of harmful sentences (0.01--0.50), harm realization (explicit vs. implicit), and the position of harmful sentences within the input (beginning, middle, end), enabling a controlled stress-test evaluation. Experiments across toxic, offensive, and hate content, and across LLaMA-3.1, Qwen-2.5, and Mistral, reveal consistent patterns: sensitivity is non-monotonic with respect to harmful prevalence, peaking at moderate levels; sensitivity degrades as input length increases; harmful sentences placed earlier in the input are more strongly prioritized; and explicit harm is more reliably identified than implicit harm. These findings provide a systematic view of how LLMs prioritize harmful sentences in long input under controlled stress conditions, highlighting both emerging strengths and remaining challenges for safety-related use."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05864","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:58:48Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:58:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-27T02:36:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05141","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05141","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05141","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Byung-Doh","familyName":"Oh","name":"Oh, Byung-Doh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tal","familyName":"Linzen","name":"Linzen, Tal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"To model human linguistic prediction, make LLMs less superhuman"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T21:53:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:00:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-26T14:52:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-27T01:06:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"When we read, we make predictions about upcoming words; these predictions influence our reading behavior. The success of large language models (LLMs), which, like humans, make predictions about upcoming words, has motivated their use as models of human linguistic prediction. Surprisingly, in the last few years, as LLMs' ability to predict the next word has improved, their ability to explain reading behavior has declined. We argue this is because current LLMs can predict upcoming words much better than human readers can. This 'superhumanness' is driven by LLMs' extensive training data, stronger long-term memory of training examples, and stronger short-term memory. We advocate for LLMs with human-like memory and for new experiments to measure the alignment between humans and LLMs, and outline directions towards achieving these goals."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to Trends in Cognitive Sciences"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05141","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:42:01Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:42:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-27T02:36:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05699","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05699","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05699","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng","familyName":"Tong","name":"Tong, Meng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuntao","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Yuntao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kejiang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kejiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiming","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Weiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ninghui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ninghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Membership Inference Attacks on Tokenizers of Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T09:05:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:34:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-12T12:20:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-13T02:19:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-08T07:05:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-10T01:06:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-25T05:56:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05-26T01:27:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Membership inference attacks (MIAs) are widely used to assess the privacy risks associated with machine learning models. However, when these attacks are applied to pre-trained large language models (LLMs), they encounter significant challenges, including mislabeled samples, distribution shifts, and discrepancies in model size between experimental and real-world settings. To address these limitations, we introduce tokenizers as a new attack vector for membership inference. Specifically, a tokenizer converts raw text into tokens for LLMs. Unlike full models, tokenizers can be efficiently trained from scratch, thereby avoiding the aforementioned challenges. In addition, the tokenizer's training data is typically representative of the data used to pre-train LLMs. Despite these advantages, the potential of tokenizers as an attack vector remains unexplored. To this end, we present the first study on membership leakage through tokenizers and explore five attack methods to infer dataset membership. Extensive experiments on millions of Internet samples reveal the vulnerabilities in the tokenizers of state-of-the-art LLMs. To mitigate this emerging risk, we further propose an adaptive defense. Our findings highlight tokenizers as an overlooked yet critical privacy threat, underscoring the urgent need for privacy-preserving mechanisms specifically designed for them."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear at USENIX Security Symposium 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05699","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:54:57Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:54:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-26T04:04:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05942","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05942","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05942","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hadi","familyName":"Mohammadi","name":"Mohammadi, Hadi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anastasia","familyName":"Giachanou","name":"Giachanou, Anastasia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert A.","familyName":"Bagheri","name":"Bagheri, Robert A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EvalMORAAL: Interpretable Chain-of-Thought and LLM-as-Judge Evaluation for Moral Alignment in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T13:52:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:49:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T08:03:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:32:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-19T21:16:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-21T00:13:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present EvalMORAAL, a transparent chain-of-thought (CoT) framework that uses two scoring methods (log-probabilities and direct ratings) plus a model-as-judge peer review to evaluate moral alignment in 20 large language models. We assess models on the World Values Survey (55 countries, 19 topics) and the PEW Global Attitudes Survey (39 countries, 8 topics). With EvalMORAAL, top models align closely with survey responses (Pearson's $r \\approx 0.90$ on WVS). Yet we find a clear regional difference: Western regions average $r=0.82$ while non-Western regions average $r=0.61$ (a 0.21 absolute gap), indicating a persistent regional alignment gap. Our framework adds three parts: (1) two scoring methods for all models to enable fair comparison, (2) a structured CoT protocol with self-consistency checks, and (3) a model-as-judge peer review that flags 348 conflicts using a data-driven threshold. Peer agreement relates to WVS survey alignment ($r=0.74$, $p\u0026lt;.001$; PEW $r=0.39$, n.s.), supporting automated quality checks. These results show real progress toward culture-aware AI while highlighting open challenges for use across regions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted as a poster at *SEM 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05942","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:00:40Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:00:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-21T02:35:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04905","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04905","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04905","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yicheng","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Yicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuante","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yuante","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yepang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yepang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Retrieval-Augmented Code Generation: A Survey with Focus on Repository-Level Approaches"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T15:20:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:40:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-25T16:58:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-27T01:56:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-20T17:52:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-21T01:16:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have significantly improved automated code generation. While existing approaches have achieved strong performance at the function and file levels, real-world software engineering requires reasoning over entire repositories, including cross-file dependencies, evolving execution environments, and global semantic consistency. This challenge has led to the emergence of Repository-Level Code Generation (RLCG), where models must retrieve, organize, and utilize repository-scale context to generate coherent and executable code changes. To address these challenges, Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) has become an increasingly important paradigm for repository-level code intelligence. In this survey, we present a comprehensive review of Retrieval-Augmented Code Generation (RACG), with a particular focus on repository-level approaches. Rather than viewing RACG as a static ``retrieve-then-generate'' pipeline, we characterize it as a coupled and evolving process involving context construction, retrieval optimization, generation, and environment interaction. We organize existing methods through a unified analytical framework spanning retrieval substrate, control regime, and evaluation setting. Based on this framework, we systematically examine retrieval strategies, graph-based and non-graph-based retrieval paradigms, training-driven optimizations, and autonomous agent architectures. We further summarize widely used datasets, benchmarks, and system configurations, and discuss key challenges including scalability, reliability, efficiency, and the necessity boundary between RACG and long-context LLMs. Through this survey, we aim to provide a structured understanding of the rapidly evolving RACG landscape and highlight promising directions for future AI-powered software engineering research."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04905","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:58:10Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:58:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-21T02:35:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05746","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05746","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05746","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bohan","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Bohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiva Krishna Reddy","familyName":"Malay","name":"Malay, Shiva Krishna Reddy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vikas","familyName":"Yadav","name":"Yadav, Vikas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ARM: Discovering Agentic Reasoning Modules for Generalizable Multi-Agent Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T10:04:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:38:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-19T06:43:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-20T00:38:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Model (LLM)-powered Multi-agent systems (MAS) have achieved state-of-the-art results on various complex reasoning tasks. Recent works have proposed techniques to automate the design of MASes, eliminating the need for manual engineering. However, these techniques perform poorly, often achieving similar or inferior performance to simple baselines. Furthermore, they require computationally expensive re-discovery of architectures for each new task domain and expensive data annotation on domains without existing labeled validation sets. A critical insight is that simple Chain of Thought (CoT) reasoning often performs competitively with these complex systems, suggesting that the fundamental reasoning unit of MASes, CoT, warrants further investigation. To this end, we present a new paradigm for automatic MAS design that pivots the focus to optimizing CoT reasoning. We introduce the Agentic Reasoning Module (ARM), an agentic generalization of CoT where each granular reasoning step is executed by a specialized reasoning module. This module is discovered through a tree search over the code space, starting from a simple CoT module and evolved using mutations informed by reflection on execution traces. The resulting ARM acts as a versatile reasoning building block which can be utilized as a direct recursive loop or as a subroutine in a learned meta-orchestrator. Our approach significantly outperforms both manually designed MASes and state-of-the-art automatic MAS design methods. Crucially, MASes built with ARM exhibit superb generalization, maintaining high performance across different foundation models and task domains without further optimization."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"29 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05746","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:56:02Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:56:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-20T02:46:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05431","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05431","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05431","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongmin","familyName":"Yoo","name":"Yoo, Yongmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Longbing","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Longbing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Self-Filtered Distillation with LLMs-generated Trust Indicators for Reliable Patent Classification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T22:50:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:14:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T05:04:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T01:31:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-05T22:50:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-07T01:10:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-19T05:55:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05-20T00:36:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Organizing large-scale patent corpora according to classification schemes is a core information management task that determines the accuracy and efficiency of prior art retrieval, technology knowledge discovery, and intellectual property decision-making. Recent approaches distill natural language rationales generated by large language models (LLMs) into compact student models, yet logical errors, label mismatches, and taxonomy misalignments inherent in these rationales are indiscriminately absorbed during training, undermining classification reliability and propagating errors throughout downstream information processes. Rather than correcting such errors post-hoc, we propose Self-Filtered Distillation (SFD), which embeds quality assurance directly into the learning process by reinterpreting LLM-generated rationales as trust indicators rather than ground-truth supervision. SFD integrates three unsupervised signals into a unified trust score that dynamically modulates each training instance's contribution: Self-Consistency, which quantifies agreement among independently generated rationales; Class Entailment Alignment, which evaluates semantic coherence between a rationale and its assigned CPC class definition; and LLM Agreement Scoring, which assesses external plausibility through an independent verifier. On the USPTO-2M benchmark comprising over two million patents, SFD achieves up to 38.7\\% relative improvement in Macro-F1 across four student architectures, and the strong correlation between trust scores and expert judgments ($r = 0.685$) confirms that the framework provides not only accurate predictions but also decomposable confidence semantics that enable auditable and self-documenting classification outcomes for large-scale patent knowledge organization."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05431","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:48:45Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:48:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-20T02:46:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05921","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05921","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05921","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hsien-Chin","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Hsien-Chin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin Matthias","familyName":"Ruppik","name":"Ruppik, Benjamin Matthias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carel","familyName":"van Niekerk","name":"van Niekerk, Carel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chia-Hao","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Chia-Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Heck","name":"Heck, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nurul","familyName":"Lubis","name":"Lubis, Nurul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renato","familyName":"Vukovic","name":"Vukovic, Renato","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shutong","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Shutong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Milica","familyName":"Gašić","name":"Gašić, Milica","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Prompt reinforcing for long-term planning of large language models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T13:30:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:48:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-09T10:26:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-10T00:44:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-17T12:12:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-19T01:08:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable success in a wide range of natural language processing tasks and can be adapted through prompting. However, they remain suboptimal in multi-turn interactions, often relying on incorrect early assumptions and failing to track user goals over time, which makes such tasks particularly challenging. Prior works in dialogue systems have shown that long-term planning is essential for handling interactive tasks. In this work, we propose a prompt optimisation framework inspired by reinforcement learning, which enables such planning to take place by only modifying the task instruction prompt of the LLM-based agent. By generating turn-by-turn feedback and leveraging experience replay for prompt rewriting, our proposed method shows significant improvement in multi-turn tasks such as text-to-SQL and task-oriented dialogue. Moreover, it generalises across different LLM-based agents and can leverage diverse LLMs as meta-prompting agents. This warrants future research in reinforcement learning-inspired parameter-free optimisation methods."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05921","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:00:10Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:00:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-19T04:48:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06062","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06062","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06062","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiakang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiakang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runze","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Runze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingpeng","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Qingpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenping","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Wenping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fuzheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Fuzheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guorui","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Guorui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Gai","name":"Gai, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ling","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Ling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When Importance Sampling Misallocates Credit: Asymmetric Ratios for Outcome-Supervised RL"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:54:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:56:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-15T05:25:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-18T00:27:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning (RL) has shown great promise in large language models (LLMs) post-training, which typically rely on token-level clipping to maintain stability during optimization. Despite the empirical success of GRPO-style methods, we identify a fundamental and previously overlooked challenge in this popular Outcome-Supervised RL (OSRL) paradigm. We reveal that in OSRL, where advantages are shared across tokens within a response, importance sampling (IS) ratios deviate from their traditional purpose of distribution correction as in classic RL, which become token-level weights that allocate the shared advantage signal across tokens. We show that this hidden role shift induces a critical mismatch for positive-advantage tokens, leading to unbalanced token weighting between positive and negative tokens. Specifically, it suppresses the update of underrepresented tokens that are lagging behind, while over-amplifying already high-probability tokens. This mismatch results in rich-get-richer dynamics that over-reinforce confident tokens, weaken catch-up learning that drive entropy collapse, excessive repetition, and premature convergence. To address this, we propose Asymmetric Importance Sampling Policy Optimization (ASPO), a simple yet effective strategy that reverses the ratio-induced weighting of positive-advantage tokens, while stabilizing extreme updates and maintaining gradient flow. This mismatch correction aligns their update direction with the learning dynamics of negative ones. Comprehensive experiments across math reasoning and coding benchmarks demonstrate that ASPO significantly mitigates entropy collapse, improves training stability, and enhances performance over strong GRPO-based baselines. Our analysis provides new insights into the role of token-level weighting in OSRL and highlights the critical importance of correcting ratio-induced weighting in LLM RL."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06062","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:03:30Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:03:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-18T02:13:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05213","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05213","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05213","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixiao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yixiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingxiao","familyName":"Huo","name":"Huo, Mingxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhixuan","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Zhixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yushi","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Yushi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingfeng","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Lingfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haotian","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Haotian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinghuan","familyName":"Shang","name":"Shang, Jinghuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chensheng","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Chensheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohit","familyName":"Bansal","name":"Bansal, Mohit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyu","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Mingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masayoshi","familyName":"Tomizuka","name":"Tomizuka, Masayoshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VER: Vision Expert Transformer for Robot Learning via Foundation Distillation and Dynamic Routing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T18:00:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:02:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-14T16:57:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-15T01:12:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pretrained vision foundation models (VFMs) advance robotic learning via rich visual representations, yet individual VFMs typically excel only in specific domains, limiting generality across tasks. Distilling multiple VFMs into a unified representation for policy can mitigate this limitation but often yields inflexible task-specific feature selection and requires costly full re-training to incorporate robot-domain knowledge. We propose VER, a Vision Expert transformer for Robot learning. During pretraining, VER distills multiple VFMs into a vision expert library. It then fine-tunes only a lightweight routing network (fewer than 0.4% of parameters) to dynamically select task-relevant experts from the pretrained library for downstream robot tasks. We further introduce Patchwise Expert Routing with Curriculum Top-K Annealing to improve both flexibility and precision of dynamic expert selection. Moreover, VER supports parameter-efficient finetuning for scalable expert utilization and adaptive robot-domain knowledge integration. Across 17 diverse robotic tasks and multiple policy heads, VER achieves state-of-the-art performance. We find that VER reduces large-norm outliers in task-irrelevant regions (e.g., background) and concentrates on task-critical regions. Visualizations and codes can be found in https://yixiaowang7.github.io/ver_page/."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05213","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:43:42Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:43:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-15T02:35:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05497","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05497","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05497","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongkai","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Zhongkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Zihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Chenyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengding","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Zhengding","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuyi","familyName":"Pei","name":"Pei, Shuyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangwook","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Yangwook","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yufei","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Yufei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Po-An","familyName":"Tsai","name":"Tsai, Po-An","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Patterns behind Chaos: Forecasting Data Movement for Efficient Large-Scale MoE LLM Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T01:31:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:17:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-04T09:23:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-05T01:31:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-05T07:59:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-08T01:23:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-02T18:32:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-06T00:04:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05-12T10:04:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-05-13T00:54:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large-scale Mixture of Experts (MoE) Large Language Models (LLMs) have recently become the frontier open-weight models, achieving remarkable model capability similar to proprietary ones. But their random expert selection mechanism introduces significant data movement overhead that becomes the dominant bottleneck in multi-unit LLM serving systems.\n To understand the patterns underlying this data movement, we conduct comprehensive data-movement-centric profiling across four state-of-the-art large-scale MoE models released in 2025 (200B-1000B) using over 24,000 requests spanning diverse workloads. We perform systematic analysis from both temporal and spatial perspectives and distill six key insights to guide the design of diverse serving systems. We verify these insights on both future wafer-scale GPU architectures and existing GPU systems. On wafer-scale GPUs, lightweight architectural modifications guided by our insights yield a 6.6$\\times$ average speedup across four 200B--1000B models. On existing GPU systems, our insights drive the design of a prefill-aware expert placement algorithm that achieves up to 1.25$\\times$ speedup on MoE computation. Our work presents the first comprehensive data-centric analysis of large-scale MoE models together with a concrete design study applying the learned lessons. Our profiling traces are publicly available at \\href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/core12345/MoE_expert_selection_trace}{\\textcolor{blue}{https://huggingface.co/datasets/core12345/MoE\\_expert\\_selection\\_trace}}."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05497","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:50:14Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:50:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-13T02:57:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05408","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05408","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05408","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kebin","familyName":"Contreras","name":"Contreras, Kebin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luis","familyName":"Toscano-Palomino","name":"Toscano-Palomino, Luis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mauro Dalla","familyName":"Mura","name":"Mura, Mauro Dalla","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jorge","familyName":"Bacca","name":"Bacca, Jorge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"See the past: Time-Reversed Scene Reconstruction from Thermal Traces Using Visual Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T21:57:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:12:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-11T20:46:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-13T00:11:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recovering the past from present observations is an intriguing challenge with potential applications in forensics and scene analysis. Thermal imaging, operating in the infrared range, provides access to otherwise invisible information. Since humans are typically warmer (37 C -98.6 F) than their surroundings, interactions such as sitting, touching, or leaning leave residual heat traces. These fading imprints serve as passive temporal codes, allowing for the inference of recent events that exceed the capabilities of RGB cameras. This work proposes a time-reversed reconstruction framework that uses paired RGB and thermal images to recover scene states from a few seconds earlier. The proposed approach couples Visual-Language Models (VLMs) with a constrained diffusion process, where one VLM generates scene descriptions and another guides image reconstruction, ensuring semantic and structural consistency. The method is evaluated in three controlled scenarios, demonstrating the feasibility of reconstructing plausible past frames up to 120 seconds earlier, providing a first step toward time-reversed imaging from thermal traces."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05408","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:48:13Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:48:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-13T02:57:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04850","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04850","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04850","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengxiang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hengxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyeong Kyu","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Hyeong Kyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sharon","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Sharon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongxin","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Hongxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Detecting Distillation Data from Reasoning Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T14:37:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:37:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-15T08:23:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-16T00:34:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-08T06:36:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-11T00:39:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reasoning distillation has emerged as a prevailing paradigm for transferring reasoning capabilities from large reasoning models to small language models. Yet, reasoning distillation risks data contamination: benchmark data may inadvertently be included in the distillation data, thereby inflating model performance metrics. In this work, we formally define the distillation data detection task, which determines whether a given question is included in the model's distillation data. The unique challenge of this task lies in the partial availability of distillation data. To address this, we propose Token Probability Deviation (TPD), a detection method that leverages the probability patterns of output tokens generated by the model instead of input tokens. Our method is motivated by the observation that seen questions tend to elicit more near-deterministic tokens generated by the models than unseen ones. Our TPD score is thus designed to quantify the token-level deviation of generated tokens from a high-confidence reference probability. Consequently, seen questions can yield substantially lower TPD scores than unseen ones, enabling strong detection performance. Extensive experiments demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach, improving detection AUC by up to 31% on distillation datasets."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04850","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:55:57Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:55:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-11T02:42:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05830","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05830","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05830","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Johnnatan","familyName":"Messias","name":"Messias, Johnnatan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ayae","familyName":"Ide","name":"Ide, Ayae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fairness in Token Delegation: Mitigating Voting Power Concentration in DAOs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T11:53:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:42:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-07T13:28:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-08T01:00:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Decentralized Autonomous Organizations (DAOs) aim to enable participatory governance, but in practice face challenges of voter apathy, concentration of voting power, and misaligned delegation. Existing delegation mechanisms often reinforce visibility biases, where a small set of highly ranked delegates accumulate disproportionate influence regardless of their alignment with the broader community. In this paper, we conduct an empirical study of delegation in DAO governance off-chain discussions from 14 DAO forums. We develop a methodology to link forum participants to on-chain addresses, extract governance interests using large language models, and compare these interests against delegates' historical behavior. Our analysis reveals that delegations are frequently misaligned with token holders' expressed priorities and that current ranking-based interfaces exacerbate power concentration. We argue that incorporating interest alignment into delegation processes could mitigate these imbalances and improve the representativeness of DAO decision-making. To support future research, we will release our dataset and code in a public repository."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05830","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:57:59Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:58:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-08T12:51:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05950","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05950","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05950","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Songyuan","familyName":"Sui","name":"Sui, Songyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zihang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xia","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Training-Free Time Series Classification via In-Context Reasoning with LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T14:07:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:50:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-30T21:41:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-04T00:11:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Time series classification (TSC) spans diverse application scenarios, yet labeled data are often scarce, making task-specific training costly and inflexible. Recent reasoning-oriented large language models (LLMs) show promise in understanding temporal patterns, but purely zero-shot usage remains suboptimal. We propose FETA, a multi-agent framework for training-free TSC via exemplar-based in-context reasoning. FETA decomposes a multivariate series into channel-wise subproblems, retrieves a few structurally similar labeled examples for each channel, and leverages a reasoning LLM to compare the query against these exemplars, producing channel-level labels with self-assessed confidences; a confidence-weighted aggregator then fuses all channel decisions. This design eliminates the need for pretraining or fine-tuning, improves efficiency by pruning irrelevant channels and controlling input length, and enhances interpretability through exemplar grounding and confidence estimation. On nine challenging UEA datasets, FETA achieves strong accuracy under a fully training-free setting, surpassing multiple trained baselines. These results demonstrate that a multi-agent in-context reasoning framework can transform LLMs into competitive, plug-and-play TSC solvers without any parameter training. The code is available at https://github.com/SongyuanSui/FETATSC."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages main content, 12 pages total including appendix, 1 figure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05950","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:00:51Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:00:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-04T01:54:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05583","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05583","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05583","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arindam","familyName":"Chowdhury","name":"Chowdhury, Arindam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Massimiliano Lupo","familyName":"Pasini","name":"Pasini, Massimiliano Lupo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When Does Global Attention Help? A Unified Empirical Study on Atomistic Graph Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.6; I.2.8; I.2.10; I.2.11","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T07, 68T09","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T05:01:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:25:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-31T17:39:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-04T00:27:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1186/s13321-026-01171-z","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Graph neural networks (GNNs) are widely used as surrogates for costly experiments and first-principles simulations to study the behavior of compounds at atomistic scale, and their architectural complexity is constantly increasing to enable the modeling of complex physics. While most recent GNNs combine more traditional message passing neural networks (MPNNs) layers to model short-range interactions with more advanced graph transformers (GTs) with global attention mechanisms to model long-range interactions, it is still unclear when global attention mechanisms provide real benefits over well-tuned MPNN layers due to inconsistent implementations, features, or hyperparameter tuning. We introduce the first unified, reproducible benchmarking framework - built on HydraGNN - that enables seamless switching among four controlled model classes: MPNN, MPNN with chemistry/topology encoders, GPS-style hybrids of MPNN with global attention, and fully fused local-global models with encoders. Using seven diverse open-source datasets for benchmarking across regression and classification tasks, we systematically isolate the contributions of message passing, global attention, and encoder-based feature augmentation. Our study shows that encoder-augmented MPNNs form a robust baseline, while fused local-global models yield the clearest benefits for properties governed by long-range interaction effects. We further quantify the accuracy-compute trade-offs of attention, reporting its overhead in memory. Together, these results establish the first controlled evaluation of global attention in atomistic graph learning and provide a reproducible testbed for future model development."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"44 pages, 8 figures, 19 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05583","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-08T01:52:15Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:52:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-04T01:54:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05192","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05192","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05192","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesca","familyName":"Gomez","name":"Gomez, Francesca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From surveillance to signalling: escalation channels as environmental controls for agentic AI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T13:37:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:02:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-29T21:49:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-01T00:11:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"When AI agents operating with access to sensitive information encounter a conflict between completing an assigned task and following rules or ethical constraints, they can resort to unsanctioned behaviour. Existing inference time safety work addresses this primarily through monitoring and access restriction. We investigate a complementary and under-explored layer: environmental controls that act on the agent's decision context at the point of conflict, making it more likely that the agent takes an authorised alternative path rather than an unsanctioned one. Drawing on Situational Crime Prevention (SCP), a framework used in human insider risk management to make harmful actions less rewarding and compliant actions more viable by design choices in the environment, we design and evaluate escalation channels as a concrete instantiation of this control class. An escalation channel provides an agent with a formal, out-of-band route to surface a conflict to an independent authority. We evaluate two designs: a simple email escalation and an instrumentally credible channel that guarantees a 30-minute pause and independent review, making the authorised path genuinely useful for goal achievement rather than merely nominally available. Across 10 frontier LLMs using the agentic task-rule conflict scenario of Lynch et al. (2025), we find that without any control the harmful action rate is 38.73%. A simple escalation channel reduces this to 5.92%; the instrumentally credible channel reduces it further to 1.21%, a statistically significant improvement observed in all 10 models tested across 24,000 samples. Our results suggest that the instrumental credibility of the authorised alternative matters considerably, and that environmental control design is a productive and largely unexplored addition to the defence-in-depth toolkit for agentic AI systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05192","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:43:12Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:43:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-01T02:32:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06002","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06002","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06002","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hudson","familyName":"de Martim","name":"de Martim, Hudson","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Deterministic Legal Agents: A Canonical Primitive API for Auditable Reasoning over Temporal Knowledge Graphs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:04:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:53:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-04T16:44:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-05T01:56:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-28T19:28:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-30T00:05:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In high-stakes legal domains, retrieval must preserve not only semantic relevance, but also the hierarchy, temporality, and causal provenance of legal norms. Standard Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), based mainly on semantic similarity over text fragments, cannot reliably provide this level of control. Prior work on SAT-Graph RAG addressed the representation problem by modeling legal materials as structure-aware temporal knowledge graphs. This paper addresses the next problem: how an LLM-based reasoning agent can interact with such a graph without reintroducing unreliable retrieval behavior.\n We specify the SAT-Graph API, a canonical primitive interface for auditable reasoning over temporal knowledge graphs, developed and illustrated in the legal domain. The API exposes typed, atomic, and composable primitives that mediate between a probabilistic language model and a deterministic symbolic substrate. Its design follows Probability Isolation: uncertainty is confined to intent translation, semantic anchoring, and final narrative synthesis, while structural, temporal, and causal graph traversals are executed through deterministic operations over canonical anchors.\n The interface shifts legal RAG from single-shot Retrieve-then-Generate to active Reason-Act-Observe. An agent decomposes a legal question into an explicit execution plan, invokes primitives for point-in-time retrieval, context reconstruction, provenance tracing, and impact analysis, and produces an answer grounded in an auditable log of graph operations. The result is a formal architectural specification, not an empirical benchmark: a secure interaction protocol that decouples legal knowledge representation from agentic reasoning. Although illustrated in law, the primitive model is domain-portable to other temporally versioned, provenance-sensitive, and authority-governed knowledge bases."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Substantially revised version consolidating the paper as a formal SAT-Graph API specification: clarifies Probability Isolation and post-anchoring determinism, broadens semantic anchoring to open and thematic legal queries, refines the data models and temporal primitives, and strengthens the use cases, limitations, and bibliography"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06002","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:02:04Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:02:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-30T02:19:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05432","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05432","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05432","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shambhavi","familyName":"Mishra","name":"Mishra, Shambhavi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gaurav","familyName":"Sahu","name":"Sahu, Gaurav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Pedersoli","name":"Pedersoli, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Laurent","familyName":"Charlin","name":"Charlin, Laurent","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jose","familyName":"Dolz","name":"Dolz, Jose","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher","familyName":"Pal","name":"Pal, Christopher","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AInstein: Can LLMs Solve Research Problems From Parametric Memory Alone?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T22:50:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:14:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-28T12:36:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-29T00:49:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Can large language models solve AI research problems using only their parametric knowledge, without fine-tuning, retrieval, or other external aids? We introduce AInstein, a framework for testing whether LLM agents can generate and refine solutions to research problems through iterative critique loops. A blind study with 20 domain experts on held-out ICLR 2026 problems validates our automated metrics, which we then scale to 1,214 ICLR 2025 papers using an LLM-as-a-judge paradigm. Two metrics capture complementary aspects of performance: Success Rate (does the solution address the problem?) and Rediscovery (does it match the published approach?). LLMs succeed on over 70% of problems, yet strictly rediscover the published solution less than 19% of the time, suggesting genuine problem-solving rather than associative recall. However, this ability has clear limits: models handle familiar methodological territory well but fail when solutions require cross-domain analogical transfer, a pattern we call the parametric knowledge boundary. On the ResearchPlanGen benchmark (2,645 problems), our training-free iterative refinement strategy matches RL finetuning, and a criteria-coverage analysis pins down the ceiling of what test-time refinement alone can achieve. Together, these findings map both the capabilities and the limits of LLMs as autonomous scientific problem-solvers."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05432","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:48:46Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:48:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-29T02:32:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05799","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05799","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05799","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rikuto","familyName":"Kotoge","name":"Kotoge, Rikuto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuichi","familyName":"Sasaki","name":"Sasaki, Yuichi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Data-efficient Targeted Token-level Preference Optimization for LLM-based Text-to-Speech"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T11:18:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:41:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-27T06:38:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-28T01:20:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Aligning text-to-speech (TTS) system outputs with human feedback through preference optimization has been shown to effectively improve the robustness and naturalness of language model-based TTS models. Current approaches primarily require paired desirable and undesirable samples at the utterance level. However, such pairs are often limited in TTS output data, and utterance-level formulation prevents fine-grained token-level optimization needed for accurate pronunciation alignment. In this study, we propose TKTO that eliminates the need for paired data, enabling a more data-efficient training paradigm, and directly targets token-level units, automatically providing fine-grained alignment signals without token-level annotations. TKTO improves the challenging Japanese TTS accuracy by 39% and reduces CER by 54%, automatically assigning 12.8 times stronger reward to targeted tokens."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ACL 2026 (Main)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05799","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:57:16Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:57:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-28T04:05:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05971","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05971","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05971","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ron","familyName":"Keuth","name":"Keuth, Ron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul","familyName":"Kaftan","name":"Kaftan, Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mattias P.","familyName":"Heinrich","name":"Heinrich, Mattias P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Shaken or Stirred? An Analysis of MetaFormer's Token Mixing for Medical Imaging"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T14:28:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:51:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-20T11:01:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-21T01:23:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-24T10:53:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-27T00:34:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The generalization of the Transformer architecture via MetaFormer has reshaped our understanding of its success in computer vision. By replacing self-attention with simpler token mixers, MetaFormer provides strong baselines for vision tasks. However, while extensively studied on natural image datasets, its use in medical imaging remains scarce, and existing works rarely compare different token mixers, potentially overlooking more suitable designs choices. In this work, we present the first comprehensive study of token mixers for medical imaging. We systematically analyze pooling-, convolution-, and attention-based token mixers within the MetaFormer architecture on image classification (global prediction task) and semantic segmentation (dense prediction task). Our evaluation spans nine datasets (seven 2D and two 3D) covering diverse modalities and common challenges in the medical domain. Given the prevalence of pretraining from natural images to mitigate medical data scarcity, we also examine transferring pretrained weights to new token mixers. Our results show that, for classification, low-complexity token mixers (e.g. grouped convolution or pooling) are sufficient, aligning with findings on natural images. Pretrained weights remain useful in some settings despite the domain gap introduced by the new token mixer. For segmentation, we find that the local inductive bias of convolutional token mixers is essential. Grouped convolutions emerge as the preferred choice, as they reduce runtime and parameter count compared to standard convolutions, while the MetaFormer's channel-MLPs already provide the necessary cross-channel interactions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Code and data: https://github.com/multimodallearning/MetaFormerMedImaging/tree/clean_code"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05971","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:01:21Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:01:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-27T01:50:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05482","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05482","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05482","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luke","familyName":"Thompson","name":"Thompson, Luke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Davy","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Davy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dai","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Dai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Slade","familyName":"Matthews","name":"Matthews, Slade","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junbin","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Junbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andi","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Andi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ATOM: A Pretrained Neural Operator for Multitask Molecular Dynamics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T00:56:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:16:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-23T17:56:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-24T01:04:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Molecular dynamics (MD) simulations underpin modern computational drug discovery, materials science, and biochemistry. Recent machine learning models provide high-fidelity MD predictions without the need to repeatedly solve quantum mechanical forces, enabling significant speedups over conventional pipelines. Yet many such methods typically enforce strict equivariance and rely on sequential rollouts, thus limiting their flexibility and simulation efficiency. They are also commonly single-task, trained on individual molecules and fixed timeframes, which restricts generalization to unseen compounds and extended timesteps. To address these issues, we propose Atomistic Transformer Operator for Molecules (ATOM), a pretrained transformer neural operator for multitask molecular dynamics. ATOM adopts a quasi-equivariant design that requires no explicit molecular graph and employs a temporal attention mechanism, allowing for the accurate parallel decoding of multiple future states. To support operator pretraining across chemicals and timescales, we curate TG80, a large, diverse, and numerically stable MD dataset with over 2.5 million femtoseconds of trajectories across 80 compounds. ATOM achieves state-of-the-art performance on established single-task benchmarks, such as MD17, RMD17 and MD22. After multitask pretraining on TG80, ATOM shows exceptional zero-shot generalization to unseen molecules across varying time horizons. We believe ATOM represents a significant step toward accurate, efficient, and transferable molecular dynamics models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICLR2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05482","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:49:54Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:49:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-24T02:13:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05608","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05608","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05608","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuzheng","familyName":"Si","name":"Si, Shuzheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haozhe","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Haozhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kangyang","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Kangyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fanchao","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Fanchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minjia","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Minjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baobao","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Baobao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maosong","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Maosong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Goal Without a Plan Is Just a Wish: Efficient and Effective Global Planner Training for Long-Horizon Agent Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T06:10:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:27:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-20T03:25:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-21T01:36:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-21T04:33:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-22T00:32:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Agents based on large language models (LLMs) struggle with brainless trial-and-error and generating hallucinatory actions due to a lack of global planning in long-horizon tasks. In this paper, we introduce a plan-and-execute framework and propose EAGLET, an efficient and effective planner training method to enhance the executor agent's planning abilities without human effort. Specifically, we train a plug-and-play global planner through a two-step process: we first synthesize high-quality plans from an advanced LLM using our proposed homologous consensus filtering strategy, and apply fine-tuning as a cold start. Moreover, we further improve the planner with a rule-based reinforcement learning stage using a novel executor capability gain reward, ensuring it can handle task instructions of varying difficulty. Experiments on three long-horizon agent tasks show that executor agents equipped with our planner outperform existing methods, achieving new state-of-the-art performance. Meanwhile, EAGLET reduces training costs by 8x compared to RL-based baselines, and it does not require manual effort or extra training data, offering an efficient and effective solution."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ACL 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05608","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:52:50Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:52:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-22T02:30:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05188","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05188","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05188","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenda","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Wenda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanqing","familyName":"Jing","name":"Jing, Yanqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junle","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Junle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yisheng","familyName":"Lv","name":"Lv, Yisheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei-Yue","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Fei-Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Plug-and-Play Dramaturge: A Divide-and-Conquer Approach for Iterative Narrative Script Refinement via Collaborative LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T05:20:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-11T08:29:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-14T00:29:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-20T04:52:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-21T01:40:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-21T02:06:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-22T00:25:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Although LLMs have been widely adopted for creative content generation, a single-pass process often struggles to produce high-quality long narratives. How to effectively revise and improve long narrative scripts like scriptwriters remains a significant challenge, as it demands a comprehensive understanding of the entire context to identify global structural issues and local detailed flaws, as well as coordinating revisions at multiple granularities and locations. Direct modifications by LLMs typically introduce inconsistencies between local edits and the overall narrative requirements. To address these issues, we propose Dramaturge, a task and feature oriented divide-and-conquer approach powered by hierarchical multiple LLM agents. It consists of a Global Review stage to grasp the overall storyline and structural issues, a Scene-level Review stage to pinpoint detailed scene and sentence flaws, and a Hierarchical Coordinated Revision stage that coordinates and integrates structural and detailed improvements throughout the script. The top-down task flow ensures that high-level strategies guide local modifications, maintaining contextual consistency. The review and revision workflow follows a coarse-to-fine iterative process, continuing through multiple rounds until no further substantive improvements can be made. Comprehensive experiments show that Dramaturge significantly outperforms all baselines in terms of script-level overall quality and scene-level details. Our approach is plug-and-play and can be easily integrated into existing methods to improve the generated scripts."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05188","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:43:07Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:43:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-22T02:30:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04800","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04800","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04800","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sangmin","familyName":"Bae","name":"Bae, Sangmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bilge","familyName":"Acun","name":"Acun, Bilge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chien-Yu","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Chien-Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haroun","familyName":"Habeeb","name":"Habeeb, Haroun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seungyeon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Seungyeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junjie","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Junjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carole-Jean","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Carole-Jean","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hybrid Architectures for Language Models: Systematic Analysis and Design Insights"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T13:30:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:33:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-23T04:37:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-24T01:29:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-21T13:16:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-22T00:56:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent progress in large language models demonstrates that hybrid architectures--combining self-attention mechanisms with structured state space models like Mamba--can achieve a compelling balance between modeling quality and computational efficiency, particularly for long-context tasks. While these hybrid models show promising performance, systematic comparisons of hybridization strategies and analyses on the key factors behind their effectiveness have not been clearly shared to the community. In this work, we present a holistic evaluation of hybrid architectures based on inter-layer (sequential) or intra-layer (parallel) fusion. We comprehensively evaluate these designs across multiple dimensions: language modeling and downstream task performance, long-context capabilities, scaling analysis, and training and inference efficiency. By investigating the core characteristics of their computational primitive, we identify the most critical elements for each hybridization strategy and further propose optimal design recipes for hybrid models. Our comprehensive analysis provides practical guidance and valuable insights for developing hybrid language models, facilitating the optimization of architectural configurations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"41 pages, 8 figures, 22 tables;"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04800","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:53:57Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:53:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-22T02:30:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05336","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05336","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05336","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongan","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Yongan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianda","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Xianda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingchen","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Qingchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahao","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Jiahao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingwei","familyName":"Ni","name":"Ni, Jingwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Qiang","name":"Qiang, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiyu","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Kaiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Grant","familyName":"McKenzie","name":"McKenzie, Grant","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renee","familyName":"Sieber","name":"Sieber, Renee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fengran","familyName":"Mo","name":"Mo, Fengran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"WeatherArchive-Bench: Benchmarking Retrieval-Augmented Reasoning for Historical Weather Archives"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T19:58:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:20:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-19T18:59:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-21T01:22:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Historical archives on weather events are collections of enduring primary source records that offer rich, untapped narratives of how societies have experienced and responded to extreme weather events. These qualitative accounts provide insights into societal vulnerability and resilience that are largely absent from meteorological records, making them valuable for climate scientists to understand societal responses. However, their vast scale, noisy digitized quality, and archaic language make it difficult to transform them into structured knowledge for climate research. To address this challenge, we introduce WeatherArchive-Bench, the first benchmark for evaluating retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) systems on historical weather archives. WeatherArchive-Bench comprises two tasks: WeatherArchive-Retrieval, which measures a system's ability to locate historically relevant passages from over one million archival news segments, and WeatherArchive-Assessment, which evaluates whether Large Language Models (LLMs) can classify societal vulnerability and resilience indicators from extreme weather narratives. Extensive experiments across sparse, dense, and re-ranking retrievers, as well as a diverse set of LLMs, reveal that dense retrievers often fail on historical terminology, while LLMs frequently misinterpret vulnerability and resilience concepts. These findings highlight key limitations in reasoning about complex societal indicators and provide insights for designing more robust climate-focused RAG systems from archival contexts. The constructed dataset and evaluation framework are publicly available at https://anonymous.4open.science/r/WeatherArchive-Bench/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"accepted to the Resource Track of SIGIR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05336","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:46:35Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:46:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-21T04:50:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05486","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05486","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05486","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cassie","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Cassie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stuti","familyName":"Mohan","name":"Mohan, Stuti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Ziyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefanie","familyName":"Tellex","name":"Tellex, Stefanie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Language Model as Planner and Formalizer under Constraints"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T01:04:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:17:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-15T19:23:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-17T00:07:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLMs have been widely used in planning, either as planners to generate action sequences end-to-end, or as formalizers to represent the planning domain and problem in a formal language that can derive plans deterministically. However, both lines of work rely on standard benchmarks that include only generic and simplistic environmental specifications, leading to potential overestimation of the planning ability of LLMs and safety concerns in downstream tasks. We bridge this gap by augmenting widely used planning benchmarks with manually annotated, fine-grained, and rich natural language constraints spanning four formally defined categories. Over 4 state-of-the-art reasoning LLMs, 4 formal languages, and 4 datasets, we show that the introduction of one-sentence constraints consistently halves performance, indicating current LLMs' lack of robustness and an avenue for future research."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"In ACL 2026 main conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05486","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:49:59Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:50:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-17T02:18:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05056","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05056","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05056","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexis","familyName":"Ross","name":"Ross, Alexis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Megha","familyName":"Srivastava","name":"Srivastava, Megha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeremiah","familyName":"Blanchard","name":"Blanchard, Jeremiah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacob","familyName":"Andreas","name":"Andreas, Jacob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Modeling Student Learning with 3.8 Million Program Traces"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:37:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:48:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-15T07:25:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-16T00:33:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As programmers write code, they often edit and retry multiple times, creating rich \"interaction traces\" that reveal how they approach coding tasks and provide clues about their level of skill development. For novice programmers in particular, these traces reflect the diverse reasoning processes they employ to code, such as exploratory behavior to understand how a programming concept works, re-strategizing in response to bugs, and personalizing stylistic choices. In this work, we explore what can be learned from training language models on such reasoning traces: not just about code, but about coders, and particularly students learning to program. We introduce a dataset of over 3.8 million programming reasoning traces from users of Pencil Code, a free online educational platform used by students to learn simple programming concepts. Compared to models trained only on final programs or synthetically-generated traces, we find that models trained on real traces are stronger at modeling diverse student behavior. Through both behavioral and probing analyses, we also find that many properties of code traces, such as goal backtracking or number of comments, can be predicted from learned representations of the students who write them. Building on this result, we show that we can help students recover from mistakes by steering code generation models to identify a sequence of edits that will results in more correct code while remaining close to the original student's style. Together, our results suggest that many properties of code are properties of individual students and that training on edit traces can lead to models that are more steerable, more predictive of student behavior while programming, and better at generating programs in their final states. Code and data is available at https://github.com/meghabyte/pencilcode-public"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to 27th International Conference on AI in Education (AIED 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05056","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:04:28Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:04:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-16T02:18:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05837","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05837","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05837","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xueting","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Xueting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qizhou","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qizhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hinrich","familyName":"Schutze","name":"Schutze, Hinrich","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kam-Fai","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, Kam-Fai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EEPO: Exploration-Enhanced Policy Optimization via Sample-Then-Forget"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T12:02:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:43:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-13T12:17:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-14T01:47:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Balancing exploration and exploitation remains a central challenge in reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR) for large language models (LLMs). Current RLVR methods often overemphasize exploitation, leading to entropy collapse, diminished exploratory capacity, and ultimately limited performance gains. Although techniques that increase policy stochasticity can promote exploration, they frequently fail to escape dominant behavioral modes. This creates a self-reinforcing loop -- repeatedly sampling and rewarding dominant modes -- that further erodes exploration. We introduce Exploration-Enhanced Policy Optimization (EEPO), a framework that promotes exploration via two-stage rollouts with adaptive unlearning. In the first stage, the model generates half of the trajectories; it then undergoes a lightweight unlearning step to temporarily suppress these sampled responses, forcing the second stage to explore different regions of the output space. This sample-then-forget mechanism disrupts the self-reinforcing loop and promotes wider exploration during rollouts. Across five reasoning benchmarks, EEPO outperforms GRPO, achieving average relative gains of 24.3% on Qwen2.5-3B, 33.0% on Llama3.2-3B-Instruct, and 10.4% on Qwen3-8B-Base."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05837","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:58:10Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:58:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-14T04:18:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05125","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05125","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05125","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reza","familyName":"Shirkavand","name":"Shirkavand, Reza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaokai","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Xiaokai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Hui","name":"Hui, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heng","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Heng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michelle","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Michelle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Catalog-Native LLM: Speaking Item-ID Dialect with Less Entanglement for Recommendation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T15:30:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:00:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-11T16:35:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-14T00:41:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While collaborative filtering delivers predictive accuracy and efficiency, and Large Language Models (LLMs) enable expressive and generalizable reasoning, modern recommendation systems must bring these strengths together. Growing user expectations, such as natural-language queries and transparent explanations, further highlight the need for a unified approach. However, doing so is nontrivial. Collaborative signals are often token-efficient but semantically opaque, while LLMs are semantically rich but struggle to model implicit user preferences when trained only on textual inputs. This paper introduces Item-ID + Oral-language Mixture-of-Experts Language Model (IDIOMoE), which treats item interaction histories as a native dialect within the language space, enabling collaborative signals to be understood in the same way as natural language. By splitting the Feed Forward Network of each block of a pretrained LLM into a separate text expert and an item expert with token-type gating, our method avoids destructive interference between text and catalog modalities. IDIOMoE demonstrates strong recommendation performance across both public and proprietary datasets, while preserving the text understanding of the pretrained model."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05125","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:41:40Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:41:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-14T04:18:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05057","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05057","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05057","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Mingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiuhe","familyName":"Shu","name":"Shu, Jiuhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeju","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zeju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Canyu","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Canyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiange","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jiange","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shenyuan","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Shenyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunhua","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Chunhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"StaMo: Unsupervised Learning of Generalizable Robot Motion from Compact State Representation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:37:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:48:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-12T12:20:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-14T01:02:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A fundamental challenge in embodied intelligence is developing expressive and compact state representations for efficient world modeling and decision making. However, existing methods often fail to achieve this balance, yielding representations that are either overly redundant or lacking in task-critical information. We propose an unsupervised approach that learns a highly compressed two-token state representation using a lightweight encoder and a pre-trained Diffusion Transformer (DiT) decoder, capitalizing on its strong generative prior. Our representation is efficient, interpretable, and integrates seamlessly into existing VLA-based models, improving performance by 14.3% on LIBERO and 30% in real-world task success with minimal inference overhead. More importantly, we find that the difference between these tokens, obtained via latent interpolation, naturally serves as a highly effective latent action, which can be further decoded into executable robot actions. This emergent capability reveals that our representation captures structured dynamics without explicit supervision. We name our method StaMo for its ability to learn generalizable robotic Motion from compact State representation, which is encoded from static images, challenging the prevalent dependence to learning latent action on complex architectures and video data. The resulting latent actions also enhance policy co-training, outperforming prior methods by 10.4% with improved interpretability. Moreover, our approach scales effectively across diverse data sources, including real-world robot data, simulation, and human egocentric video."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05057","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:04:30Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:04:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-14T04:18:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05524","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05524","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05524","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kuangshi","familyName":"Ai","name":"Ai, Kuangshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan A.","familyName":"Karr","name":"Karr, Jonathan A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Meng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nitesh V.","familyName":"Chawla","name":"Chawla, Nitesh V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaoli","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chaoli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"KEO: Knowledge Extraction on OMIn via Knowledge Graphs and RAG for Safety-Critical Aviation Maintenance"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T02:29:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:19:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-08T21:13:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-10T00:10:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present Knowledge Extraction on OMIn (KEO), a domain-specific knowledge extraction and reasoning framework with large language models (LLMs) in safety-critical contexts. Using the Operations and Maintenance Intelligence (OMIn) dataset, we construct a QA benchmark spanning global sensemaking and actionable maintenance tasks. KEO builds a structured Knowledge Graph (KG) and integrates it into a retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) pipeline, enabling more coherent, dataset-wide reasoning than traditional text-chunk RAG. We evaluate locally deployable LLMs (Gemma-3, Phi-4, Mistral-Nemo) and employ stronger models (GPT-4o, Llama-3.3) as judges. Experiments show that KEO markedly improves global sensemaking by revealing patterns and system-level insights, while text-chunk RAG remains effective for fine-grained procedural tasks requiring localized retrieval. These findings underscore the promise of KG-augmented LLMs for secure, domain-specific QA and their potential in high-stakes reasoning. The code is available at https://github.com/JonathanKarr33/keo."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05524","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:50:52Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:50:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-10T02:24:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05038","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05038","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05038","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Omri","familyName":"Uzan","name":"Uzan, Omri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Asaf","familyName":"Yehudai","name":"Yehudai, Asaf","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roi","familyName":"pony","name":"pony, Roi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eyal","familyName":"Shnarch","name":"Shnarch, Eyal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ariel","familyName":"Gera","name":"Gera, Ariel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Guided Query Refinement: Multimodal Hybrid Retrieval with Test-Time Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:12:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:46:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-05T19:30:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-09T01:04:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-06T22:22:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-08T00:11:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal encoders have pushed the boundaries of visual document retrieval, matching textual query tokens directly to image patches and achieving state-of-the-art performance on public benchmarks. Recent models relying on this paradigm have massively scaled the sizes of their query and document representations, presenting obstacles to deployment and scalability in real-world pipelines. Furthermore, purely vision-centric approaches may be constrained by the inherent modality gap still exhibited by modern vision-language models. In this work, we connect these challenges to the paradigm of hybrid retrieval, investigating whether a lightweight dense text retriever can enhance a stronger vision-centric model. Existing hybrid methods, which rely on coarse-grained fusion of ranks or scores, fail to exploit the rich interactions within each model's representation space. To address this, we introduce Guided Query Refinement (GQR), a novel test-time optimization method that refines a primary retriever's query embedding using guidance from a complementary retriever's scores. Through extensive experiments on visual document retrieval benchmarks, we demonstrate that GQR allows vision-centric models to match the performance of models with significantly larger representations, while being up to 14x faster and requiring 54x less memory. Our findings show that GQR effectively pushes the Pareto frontier for performance and efficiency in multimodal retrieval. We release our code at https://github.com/IBM/test-time-hybrid-retrieval"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05038","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:03:44Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:03:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-08T02:31:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05026","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05026","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05026","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Beauchemin","name":"Beauchemin, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Tremblay","name":"Tremblay, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamed Amine","familyName":"Youssef","name":"Youssef, Mohamed Amine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard","familyName":"Khoury","name":"Khoury, Richard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Idiom Understanding as a Tool to Measure the Dialect Gap"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:04:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:46:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-03T15:48:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-04T01:59:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-07T12:32:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-10T00:42:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-06T21:13:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-08T00:09:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The tasks of idiom understanding and dialect understanding are both well-established benchmarks in natural language processing. In this paper, we propose combining them, and using regional idioms as a test of dialect understanding. Towards this end, we propose three new benchmark datasets for the Quebec dialect of French: QFrCoRE, which contains 4,633 instances of idiomatic phrases, and QFrCoRT, which comprises 171 regional instances of idiomatic words, and a new benchmark for French Metropolitan expressions, MFrCoE, which comprises 4,938 phrases. We explain how to construct these corpora, so that our methodology can be replicated for other dialects. Our experiments with 111 LLMs reveal a critical disparity in dialectal competence: while models perform well on French Metropolitan, 65.77% of them perform significantly worse on Quebec idioms, with only 9.0% favoring the regional dialect. These results confirm that our benchmarks are a reliable tool for quantifying the dialect gap and that prestige-language proficiency does not guarantee regional dialect understanding."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ACL 2026 findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05026","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:03:14Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:03:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-08T02:31:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05528","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05528","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05528","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lawrence","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Lawrence","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengdi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Mengdi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tuo","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Tuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin F.","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Lin F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ARMOR: High-Performance Semi-Structured Pruning via Adaptive Matrix Factorization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T02:39:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:20:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-03T05:57:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-06T00:24:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) present significant deployment challenges due to their immense computational and memory requirements. While semi-structured pruning, particularly 2:4 sparsity, offers a path to practical hardware acceleration, existing methods often incur substantial performance degradation. To bridge this gap, we introduce ARMOR: (Adaptive Representation with Matrix-factORization), a novel one-shot post-training pruning algorithm. Instead of directly pruning weights, ARMOR factorizes each weight matrix into a 2:4 sparse core wrapped by two low-overhead, block diagonal matrices. These wrappers act as efficient pre and post-transformation error correctors, offering greater flexibility to preserve model quality compared to conventional 2:4 pruning techniques. The sparse core and block diagonal wrappers are chosen through a block coordinate descent algorithm that minimizes a layer-wise proxy loss. We theoretically prove this optimization is guaranteed to converge to a solution with a proxy loss less than or equal to state-of-the-art pruning algorithms. Experiments on Llama (Touvron et al., 2023; Dubey et al., 2024) and Qwen (Yang et al., 2025) model families demonstrate that ARMOR consistently and significantly outperforms state-of-the-art 2:4 pruning methods across a wide range of downstream tasks and perplexity evaluations. ARMOR achieves this superior performance while retaining the inference speedups and substantial memory usage reductions of 2:4 pruning, establishing a more effective trade-off between model compression and task accuracy"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026, code: https://github.com/LawrenceRLiu/ARMOR"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05528","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:50:58Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:50:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-06T01:49:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04923","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04923","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04923","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alec K.","familyName":"Peltekian","name":"Peltekian, Alec K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Halil Ertugrul","familyName":"Aktas","name":"Aktas, Halil Ertugrul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gorkem","familyName":"Durak","name":"Durak, Gorkem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Grudzinski","name":"Grudzinski, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bradford C.","familyName":"Bemiss","name":"Bemiss, Bradford C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carrie","familyName":"Richardson","name":"Richardson, Carrie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jane E.","familyName":"Dematte","name":"Dematte, Jane E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"G. R. Scott","familyName":"Budinger","name":"Budinger, G. R. Scott","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anthony J.","familyName":"Esposito","name":"Esposito, Anthony J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Misharin","name":"Misharin, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alok","familyName":"Choudhary","name":"Choudhary, Alok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ankit","familyName":"Agrawal","name":"Agrawal, Ankit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ulas","familyName":"Bagci","name":"Bagci, Ulas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"REN: Anatomically-Informed Mixture-of-Experts for Interstitial Lung Disease Diagnosis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T15:35:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:41:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-11T14:41:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-13T01:49:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-30T22:54:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-01T00:12:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architectures achieve scalable learning by routing inputs to specialized subnetworks through conditional computation. However, conventional MoE designs assume homogeneous expert capability and domain-agnostic routing-assumptions that are fundamentally misaligned with medical imaging, where anatomical structure and regional disease heterogeneity govern pathological patterns. We introduce Regional Expert Networks (REN), the first anatomically-informed MoE framework for medical image classification. REN encodes anatomical priors by training seven specialized experts, each dedicated to a distinct lung lobe or bilateral lung combination, enabling precise modeling of region-specific pathological variation. Multi-modal gating mechanisms dynamically integrate radiomics biomarkers with deep learning (DL) features extracted by convolutional (CNN), Transformer (ViT), and state-space (Mamba) architectures to weight expert contributions at inference. Applied to interstitial lung disease (ILD) classification on a 597-patient, 1,898-scan longitudinal cohort, REN achieves consistently superior performance: the radiomics-guided ensemble attains an average AUC of 0.8646 +- 0.0467, a +12.5 % improvement over the SwinUNETR single-model baseline (AUC 0.7685, p=0.031). Lower-lobe experts reach AUCs of 0.88-0.90, outperforming DL baselines (CNN: 0.76-0.79) and mirroring known patterns of basal ILD progression. Evaluated under rigorous patient-level cross-validation, REN demonstrates strong generalizability and clinical interpretability, establishing a scalable, anatomically-guided framework potentially extensible to other structured medical imaging tasks. Code is available on our GitHub https://github.com/NUBagciLab/MoE-REN."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 4 figures, 5 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04923","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:58:54Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:58:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-01T02:31:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06162","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06162","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06162","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher","familyName":"Kolberg","name":"Kolberg, Christopher","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jules","familyName":"Kreuer","name":"Kreuer, Jules","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonas","familyName":"Huurdeman","name":"Huurdeman, Jonas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sofiane","familyName":"Ouaari","name":"Ouaari, Sofiane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Katharina","familyName":"Eggensperger","name":"Eggensperger, Katharina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nico","familyName":"Pfeifer","name":"Pfeifer, Nico","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TabPFN-Wide: Continued Pre-Training for Extreme Feature Counts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:28:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:01:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-29T15:04:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-31T01:03:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Revealing novel insights from the relationship between molecular measurements and pathology remains a very impactful application of machine learning in biomedicine. Data in this domain typically contain only a few observations but thousands of potentially noisy features, posing challenges for conventional tabular machine learning approaches. While prior-data fitted networks emerge as foundation models for predictive tabular data tasks, they are currently not suited to handle large feature counts (\u0026gt;500). Although feature reduction enables their application, it hinders feature importance analysis. We propose a strategy that extends existing models through continued pre-training on synthetic data sampled from a customized prior. The resulting model, TabPFN-Wide, matches or exceeds its base model's performance, while exhibiting improved robustness to noise. It seamlessly scales beyond 30,000 categorical and continuous features, regardless of noise levels, while maintaining inherent interpretability, which is critical for biomedical applications. Our results demonstrate that prior-informed adaptation is suitable to enhance the capability of foundation models for high-dimensional data. On real-world omics datasets, we show that many of the most relevant features identified by the model overlap with previous biological findings, while others propose potential starting points for future studies."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06162","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:05:48Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:05:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-31T04:02:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05825","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05825","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05825","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giorgio","familyName":"Giannone","name":"Giannone, Giorgio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangxuan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Guangxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikhil Shivakumar","familyName":"Nayak","name":"Nayak, Nikhil Shivakumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rohan Mahesh","familyName":"Awhad","name":"Awhad, Rohan Mahesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shivchander","familyName":"Sudalairaj","name":"Sudalairaj, Shivchander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akash","familyName":"Srivastava","name":"Srivastava, Akash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mitigating Premature Exploitation in Particle-based Monte Carlo for Inference-Time Scaling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T11:48:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:42:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-27T20:24:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-31T00:10:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Inference-Time Scaling (ITS) improves language models by allocating more computation at generation time. Particle Filtering (PF) has emerged as a strong ITS method for complex mathematical reasoning tasks, but it is vulnerable when guided by process reward models, which often assign overconfident scores early in the reasoning process. This causes PF to suffer from premature exploitation: it myopically commits to locally promising trajectories, prunes potentially correct hypotheses, and converges to suboptimal solutions. This failure mode, known as particle impoverishment, is especially severe under constrained computational budgets. To address this, we analyze the problem and identify two root causes: a lack of diversity in the particle set due to overconfident resampling and consequent inability to assess the potential of a reasoning path. We introduce Entropic Particle Filtering (ePF), an algorithm that integrates two new techniques to solve these issues. The first technique, Entropic Annealing (EA), directly mitigates particle impoverishment by monitoring search diversity via entropy; when diversity drops, it intervenes by dynamically annealing the resampling distribution to preserve exploration. The second, an enhancement called Look-ahead Modulation (LaM), adds a predictive guide to evaluate a state's potential based on its successors. On several challenging math benchmarks, ePF significantly outperforms strong baselines and achieves up to a 50% relative improvement in task reward. Together, these methods improve PF's resilience by balancing the exploration of diverse solution spaces with the exploitation of high-reward regions, ultimately leading to higher-quality solutions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05825","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:57:52Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:57:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-31T04:02:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04900","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04900","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04900","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nick","familyName":"Janssen","name":"Janssen, Nick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Melanie","familyName":"Schaller","name":"Schaller, Melanie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bodo","familyName":"Rosenhahn","name":"Rosenhahn, Bodo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Benchmarking M-LTSF: Frequency and Noise-Based Evaluation of Multivariate Long Time Series Forecasting Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T15:16:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:40:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-26T12:36:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-27T00:51:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding the robustness of deep learning models for multivariate long-term time series forecasting (M-LTSF) remains challenging, as evaluations typically rely on real-world datasets with unknown noise properties. We propose a simulation-based evaluation framework that generates parameterizable synthetic datasets, where each dataset instance corresponds to a different configuration of signal components, noise types, signal-to-noise ratios, and frequency characteristics. These configurable components aim to model real-world multivariate time series data without the ambiguity of unknown noise. This framework enables fine-grained, systematic evaluation of M-LTSF models under controlled and diverse scenarios. We benchmark four representative architectures S-Mamba (state-space), iTransformer (transformer-based), R-Linear (linear), and Autoformer (decomposition-based). Our analysis reveals that all models degrade severely when lookback windows cannot capture complete periods of seasonal patters in the data. S-Mamba and Autoformer perform best on sawtooth patterns, while R-Linear and iTransformer favor sinusoidal signals. White and Brownian noise universally degrade performance with lower signal-to-noise ratio while S-Mamba shows specific trend-noise and iTransformer shows seasonal-noise vulnerability. Further spectral analysis shows that S-Mamba and iTransformer achieve superior frequency reconstruction. This controlled approach, based on our synthetic and principle-driven testbed, offers deeper insights into model-specific strengths and limitations through the aggregation of MSE scores and provides concrete guidance for model selection based on signal characteristics and noise conditions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Number of pages: 13 Number of figures: 16 Number of Tables: 1"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04900","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:57:58Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:57:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-27T02:22:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05318","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05318","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05318","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nan","familyName":"Huo","name":"Huo, Nan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaohan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xiaohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinyang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jinyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Per","familyName":"Jacobsson","name":"Jacobsson, Per","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shipei","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Shipei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Binyuan","familyName":"Hui","name":"Hui, Binyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaolong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiaolong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ge","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Ge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuzheng","familyName":"Si","name":"Si, Shuzheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linheng","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Linheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Edward","familyName":"Alexander","name":"Alexander, Edward","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xintong","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Xintong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruihan","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Ruihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiyao","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Yiyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feige","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Feige","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihao","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Weihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yun","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hongyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenhao","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Chenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fatma","familyName":"Ozcan","name":"Ozcan, Fatma","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yannis","familyName":"Papakonstantinou","name":"Papakonstantinou, Yannis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reynold","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Reynold","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BIRD-INTERACT: Re-imagining Text-to-SQL Evaluation for Large Language Models via Lens of Dynamic Interactions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T19:31:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:07:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T14:39:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:51:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-24T09:38:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-25T00:46:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable performance on single-turn text-to-SQL tasks, but real-world database applications predominantly require multi-turn interactions to handle ambiguous queries, execution errors, and evolving user requirements. Existing multi-turn benchmarks fall short by treating conversation histories as static context or limiting evaluation to read-only operations, failing to reflect production-grade database assistant challenges. We introduce BIRD-INTERACT, a benchmark that restores this realism through: (1) a comprehensive interaction environment coupling each database with a hierarchical knowledge base, metadata files, and a function-driven user simulator, enabling models to solicit clarifications, retrieve knowledge, and recover from errors without human supervision; (2) two evaluation settings consisting of a pre-defined conversational protocol (c-Interact) and an open-ended agentic setting (a-Interact) where models autonomously decide when to query the user simulator or explore the environment; (3) a challenging task suite covering the full CRUD spectrum for business-intelligence and operational use cases, guarded by executable test cases. Each task features ambiguous and follow-up sub-tasks requiring dynamic interaction. The suite comprises BIRD-INTERACT-FULL (600 tasks, up to 11,796 interactions) for comprehensive performance assessment, and BIRD-INTERACT-LITE (300 tasks with simplified databases) for detailed behavioral analysis and rapid method development. Our empirical results highlight BIRD-INTERACT's difficulty: GPT-5 completes only 8.67% of tasks in c-Interact and 17.00% in a-Interact. Analysis via memory grafting and Interaction Test-time Scaling validates the importance of effective interaction for complex, dynamic text-to-SQL tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026 Oral. Dataset and code available at https://bird-interact.github.io"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05318","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:46:10Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:46:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-25T02:31:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06199","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06199","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06199","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengyang","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Chengyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Uksang","familyName":"Yoo","name":"Yoo, Uksang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arkadeep Narayan","familyName":"Chaudhury","name":"Chaudhury, Arkadeep Narayan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giljoo","familyName":"Nam","name":"Nam, Giljoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan","familyName":"Francis","name":"Francis, Jonathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeffrey","familyName":"Ichnowski","name":"Ichnowski, Jeffrey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jean","familyName":"Oh","name":"Oh, Jean","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DYMO-Hair: Generalizable Volumetric Dynamics Modeling for Robot Hair Manipulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:53:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:03:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-22T00:14:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-24T00:52:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hair care is an essential daily activity, yet it remains inaccessible to individuals with limited mobility and challenging for autonomous robot systems due to the fine-grained physical structure and complex dynamics of hair. In this work, we present DYMO-Hair, a model-based robot hair care system. We introduce a novel dynamics learning paradigm that is suited for volumetric quantities such as hair, relying on an action-conditioned latent state editing mechanism, coupled with a compact 3D latent space of diverse hairstyles to improve generalizability. This latent space is pre-trained at scale using a novel hair physics simulator, enabling generalization across previously unseen hairstyles. Using the dynamics model with a Model Predictive Path Integral (MPPI) planner, DYMO-Hair is able to perform visual goal-conditioned hair styling. Experiments in simulation demonstrate that DYMO-Hair's dynamics model outperforms baselines on capturing local deformation for diverse, unseen hairstyles. DYMO-Hair further outperforms baselines in closed-loop hair styling tasks on unseen hairstyles, with an average of 22% lower final geometric error and 42% higher success rate than the state-of-the-art system. Real-world experiments exhibit zero-shot transferability of our system to wigs, achieving consistent success on challenging unseen hairstyles where the state-of-the-art system fails. Together, these results introduce a foundation for model-based robot hair care, advancing toward more generalizable, flexible, and accessible robot hair styling in unconstrained physical environments. More details are available on our project page: https://dymohair.github.io/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear in ICRA 2026. Project page: https://dymohair.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06199","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:06:42Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:06:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-24T04:16:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05092","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05092","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05092","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Avichal","familyName":"Goel","name":"Goel, Avichal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Yoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nir","familyName":"Shavit","name":"Shavit, Nir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tony T.","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Tony T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning to Interpret Weight Differences in Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:57:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:49:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-17T16:55:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-20T00:56:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-21T07:29:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-22T00:35:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-21T16:08:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-03-24T00:45:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Finetuning (pretrained) language models is a standard approach for updating their internal parametric knowledge and specializing them to new tasks and domains. However, the corresponding model weight changes (\"weight diffs\") are not generally interpretable. While inspecting the finetuning dataset can give a sense of how the model might have changed, these datasets are often not publicly available or are too large to work with directly. Towards the goal of comprehensively understanding weight diffs in natural language, we introduce Diff Interpretation Tuning (DIT), a method that trains models to describe their own finetuning-induced modifications. Our approach uses synthetic, labeled weight diffs to train a DIT-adapter, which can be applied to a compatible finetuned model to make it describe how it has changed. We demonstrate in two proof-of-concept settings (reporting hidden behaviors and summarizing finetuned knowledge) that our method enables models to describe their finetuning-induced modifications using accurate natural language descriptions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project code and links to weight diffs, adapters, and training data can be found at https://github.com/Aviously/diff-interpretation-tuning"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05092","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:05:56Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:05:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-24T04:16:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05181","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05181","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05181","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ander Artola","familyName":"Velasco","name":"Velasco, Ander Artola","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stratis","familyName":"Tsirtsis","name":"Tsirtsis, Stratis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manuel","familyName":"Gomez-Rodriguez","name":"Gomez-Rodriguez, Manuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Auditing Pay-Per-Token in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T17:47:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-23T16:07:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-24T02:08:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Millions of users rely on a market of cloud-based services to obtain access to state-of-the-art large language models. However, it has been very recently shown that the de facto pay-per-token pricing mechanism used by providers creates a financial incentive for them to strategize and misreport the (number of) tokens a model used to generate an output. In this paper, we develop an auditing framework based on martingale theory that enables a trusted third-party auditor who sequentially queries a provider to detect token misreporting. Crucially, we show that our framework is guaranteed to always detect token misreporting, regardless of the provider's (mis-)reporting policy, and not falsely flag a faithful provider as unfaithful with high probability. To validate our auditing framework, we conduct experiments across a wide range of (mis-)reporting policies using several large language models from the $\\texttt{Llama}$, $\\texttt{Gemma}$ and $\\texttt{Ministral}$ families, and input prompts from a popular crowdsourced benchmarking platform. The results show that our framework detects an unfaithful provider after observing fewer than $\\sim 70$ reported outputs, while maintaining the probability of falsely flagging a faithful provider below $α= 0.05$."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"AISTATS 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05181","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:42:57Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:42:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-24T04:16:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05710","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05710","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05710","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabrizio","familyName":"Dimino","name":"Dimino, Fabrizio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhinav","familyName":"Arun","name":"Arun, Abhinav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bhaskarjit","familyName":"Sarmah","name":"Sarmah, Bhaskarjit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefano","familyName":"Pasquali","name":"Pasquali, Stefano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FinReflectKG -- EvalBench: Benchmarking Financial KG with Multi-Dimensional Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Finance (q-fin.CP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T09:22:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:35:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-19T20:05:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-23T00:08:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly being used to extract structured knowledge from unstructured financial text. Although prior studies have explored various extraction methods, there is no universal benchmark or unified evaluation framework for the construction of financial knowledge graphs (KG). We introduce FinReflectKG - EvalBench, a benchmark and evaluation framework for KG extraction from SEC 10-K filings. Building on the agentic and holistic evaluation principles of FinReflectKG - a financial KG linking audited triples to source chunks from S\u0026amp;P 100 filings and supporting single-pass, multi-pass, and reflection-agent-based extraction modes - EvalBench implements a deterministic commit-then-justify judging protocol with explicit bias controls, mitigating position effects, leniency, verbosity and world-knowledge reliance. Each candidate triple is evaluated with binary judgments of faithfulness, precision, and relevance, while comprehensiveness is assessed on a three-level ordinal scale (good, partial, bad) at the chunk level. Our findings suggest that, when equipped with explicit bias controls, LLM-as-Judge protocols provide a reliable and cost-efficient alternative to human annotation, while also enabling structured error analysis. Reflection-based extraction emerges as the superior approach, achieving best performance in comprehensiveness, precision, and relevance, while single-pass extraction maintains the highest faithfulness. By aggregating these complementary dimensions, FinReflectKG - EvalBench enables fine-grained benchmarking and bias-aware evaluation, advancing transparency and governance in financial AI applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05710","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:55:12Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:55:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-23T02:14:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05154","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05154","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05154","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shenzhe","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Shenzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Shu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michiel A.","familyName":"Bakker","name":"Bakker, Michiel A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Pentland","name":"Pentland, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxin","familyName":"Pei","name":"Pei, Jiaxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Can AI Truly Represent Your Voice in Deliberations? A Comprehensive Study of Large-Scale Opinion Aggregation with LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T17:36:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T05:52:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:26:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-08T19:39:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-10T01:04:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-26T17:20:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02-27T01:58:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-03-20T00:11:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-03-23T00:17:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large-scale public deliberations generate thousands of free-form contributions that must be synthesized into representative and neutral summaries for policy use. While LLMs have been shown as a promising tool to generate summaries for large-scale deliberations, they also risk underrepresenting minority perspectives and exhibiting bias with respect to the input order, raising fairness concerns in high-stakes contexts. Studying and fixing these issues requires a comprehensive evaluation at a large scale, yet current practice often relies on LLMs as judges, which show weak alignment with human judgments. To address this, we present DeliberationBank, a large-scale human-grounded dataset with (1) opinion data spanning ten deliberation questions created by 3,000 participants and (2) summary judgment data annotated by 4,500 participants across four dimensions (representativeness, informativeness, neutrality, policy approval). Using these datasets, we train DeliberationJudge, a fine-tuned DeBERTa model that can rate deliberation summaries from individual perspectives. DeliberationJudge is more efficient and more aligned with human judgements compared to a wide range of LLM judges. With DeliberationJudge, we evaluate 18 LLMs and reveal persistent weaknesses in deliberation summarization, especially underrepresentation of minority positions. Our framework provides a scalable and reliable way to evaluate deliberation summarization, helping ensure AI systems are more representative and equitable for policymaking."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05154","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:42:19Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:42:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-23T02:13:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05138","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05138","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05138","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gregory Hok Tjoan","familyName":"Go","name":"Go, Gregory Hok Tjoan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Khang","familyName":"Ly","name":"Ly, Khang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anders","familyName":"Søgaard","name":"Søgaard, Anders","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amin","familyName":"Tabatabaei","name":"Tabatabaei, Amin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maarten","familyName":"de Rijke","name":"de Rijke, Maarten","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xinyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LiRA: A Multi-Agent Framework for Reliable and Readable Literature Review Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T12:14:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:00:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-25T12:22:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-26T01:49:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-30T13:35:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-01T01:30:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-20T15:51:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-03-23T01:00:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid growth of scientific publications has made it increasingly difficult to keep literature reviews comprehensive and up-to-date. Though prior work has focused on automating retrieval and screening, the writing phase of systematic reviews remains largely under-explored, especially with regard to readability and factual accuracy. To address this, we present LiRA (Literature Review Agents), a multi-agent collaborative workflow which emulates the human literature review process. LiRA utilizes specialized agents for content outlining, subsection writing, editing, and reviewing, producing cohesive and comprehensive review articles. Evaluated on SciReviewGen and a proprietary ScienceDirect dataset, LiRA outperforms current baselines such as AutoSurvey and MASS-Survey in writing and citation quality, while maintaining competitive similarity to human-written reviews. We further evaluate LiRA in real-world scenarios using document retrieval and assess its robustness to reviewer model variation. Our findings highlight the potential of agentic LLM workflows, even without domain-specific tuning, to improve the reliability and usability of automated scientific writing."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published at the 40th AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence. Please cite the published version here: https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/view/41489"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05138","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:41:57Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:41:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-23T02:13:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06107","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06107","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06107","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gagan","familyName":"Bhatia","name":"Bhatia, Gagan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Somayajulu G","familyName":"Sripada","name":"Sripada, Somayajulu G","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Allan","name":"Allan, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacobo","familyName":"Azcona","name":"Azcona, Jacobo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Distributional Semantics Tracing: A Framework for Explaining Hallucinations in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T16:40:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:58:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T18:51:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:04:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-15T15:37:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-17T01:24:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hallucinations in large language models (LLMs) produce fluent continuations that are not supported by the prompt, especially under minimal contextual cues and ambiguity. We introduce Distributional Semantics Tracing (DST), a model-native method that builds layer-wise semantic maps at the answer position by decoding residual-stream states through the unembedding, selecting a compact top-$K$ concept set, and estimating directed concept-to-concept support via lightweight causal tracing. Using these traces, we test a representation-level hypothesis: hallucinations arise from correlation-driven representational drift across depth, where the residual stream is pulled toward a locally coherent but context-inconsistent concept neighborhood reinforced by training co-occurrences. On Racing Thoughts dataset, DST yields more faithful explanations than attribution, probing, and intervention baselines under an LLM-judge protocol, and the resulting Contextual Alignment Score (CAS) strongly predicts failures, supporting this drift hypothesis."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06107","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:04:32Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:04:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-17T04:56:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05748","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05748","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05748","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hachem","familyName":"Madmoun","name":"Madmoun, Hachem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Salem","familyName":"Lahlou","name":"Lahlou, Salem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Communication Enables Cooperation in LLM Agents: A Comparison with Curriculum-Based Approaches"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T10:06:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:38:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-16T09:52:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-19T01:28:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-11T12:10:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-12T00:46:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Eliciting cooperation in multi-agent LLM systems is critical for AI alignment. We investigate two approaches: direct communication and curriculum learning. In a 4-player Stag Hunt, a one-word \"cheap talk\" channel increases cooperation from 0% to 96.7%, demonstrating communication as a robust coordination mechanism. In contrast, we find that curriculum learning is highly sensitive to design choices: our pedagogical curriculum through progressively complex games reduced agent payoffs by 27.4% in an Iterated Public Goods Game with Punishment, demonstrating that optimizing for short-term rationality can actively undermine alignment goals. Qualitative analysis reveals that curricula emphasizing defection-equilibrium games can induce \"learned pessimism\" in agents. These findings suggest that for coordination problems, simple communication protocols may be more reliable than experience-based training, and that curriculum design for social dilemmas requires careful attention to the strategic lessons embedded in game sequences."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in EACL 2026 - Corrected cooperation rates for two-stage communication conditions (96.7% and 100.0%, previously reported as 48.3% and 50.0% due to a denominator bug in the evaluation code). All other results unchanged"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05748","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:56:05Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:56:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-12T02:25:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06195","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06195","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06195","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yen-Ju","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yen-Ju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yashesh","familyName":"Gaur","name":"Gaur, Yashesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Muller","name":"Muller, Benjamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jesus","familyName":"Villalba","name":"Villalba, Jesus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Najim","familyName":"Dehak","name":"Dehak, Najim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luke","familyName":"Zettlemoyer","name":"Zettlemoyer, Luke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gargi","familyName":"Ghosh","name":"Ghosh, Gargi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mike","familyName":"Lewis","name":"Lewis, Mike","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Srinivasan","familyName":"Iyer","name":"Iyer, Srinivasan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Duc","familyName":"Le","name":"Le, Duc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Latent Speech-Text Transformer"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:52:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:03:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-09T19:57:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-11T00:07:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Auto-regressive speech-text models pre-trained on interleaved text tokens and discretized speech tokens demonstrate strong speech understanding and generation, yet remain substantially less compute-efficient than text LLMs, partly due to the much longer sequences of speech tokens relative to text. This modality imbalance disproportionately allocates pre-training and inference compute to speech, potentially hindering effective cross-modal alignment and slowing performance scaling by orders of magnitude. We introduce the Latent Speech-Text Transformer (LST), which aggregates speech tokens into latent speech patches that serve as higher-level autoregressive units. This design aligns the sequence-modeling granularity between speech and text while improving computational efficiency. The resulting patches can align with textual units to facilitate cross-modal knowledge transfer and compactly capture recurring acoustic patterns such as silence. Across story-completion benchmarks under both compute-controlled and data-controlled settings, LST consistently improves speech accuracy while also improving text performance, achieving up to +6.5% absolute gain on speech HellaSwag in compute-controlled training (+5.3% in data-controlled training). Under compute-controlled scaling from 420M to 1.8B parameters in a near compute-optimal regime, gains grow with scale, and improvements persist up to 7B parameters under fixed-token budgets. These benefits extend to downstream tasks: LST stabilizes ASR adaptation and reduces the effective autoregressive sequence length during ASR and TTS inference, lowering computational cost without degrading reconstruction quality. The code is available at https://github.com/facebookresearch/lst."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICLR 2026 (Oral)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06195","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:06:36Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:06:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-11T02:33:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05278","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05278","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05278","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paloma","familyName":"García-de-Herreros","name":"García-de-Herreros, Paloma","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philipp","familyName":"Slusallek","name":"Slusallek, Philipp","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dietrich","familyName":"Klakow","name":"Klakow, Dietrich","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vagrant","familyName":"Gautam","name":"Gautam, Vagrant","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Decoding Partial Differential Equations: Cross-Modal Adaptation of Decoder-only Models to PDEs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T18:46:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:05:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-06T09:09:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-09T00:33:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While large language models are primarily used on natural language tasks, they have also shown great promise when adapted to new modalities, e.g., for scientific machine learning tasks. Most proposed approaches for such cross-modal adaptation of language models focus on encoder-only transformer model architectures, despite decoder-only architectures being far more popular for language tasks in recent years, and being trained at much larger scales. This raises the question of how model architecture affects cross-modal adaptation approaches, and whether we can leverage the success of decoder-only models. In this paper, we systematically compare encoder-only and decoder-only language models on cross-modal adaptation for time-dependent simulation tasks based on partial differential equations (PDEs). We find that decoder-only models are far worse than encoder-only models, when existing approaches are applied unmodified. In contrast to several other domains, scaling decoder-only models also does not help. To enhance the performance of decoder-only models in this context, we introduce two novel approaches that mimic bidirectionality, Parallel Flipping and Sequence Doubling. Both our methods improve overall performance using decoder-only models for all tasks and all cross-modal adaptation methods, closing the gap to encoder-only model performance. We hope that our findings broaden the spectrum of models used on cross-modal adaptation tasks to further scientific machine learning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026 Workshop on AI and Partial Differential Equations"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05278","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:45:13Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:45:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-09T02:09:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05449","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05449","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05449","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew","familyName":"Jörke","name":"Jörke, Matthew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Defne","familyName":"Genç","name":"Genç, Defne","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Valentin","familyName":"Teutschbein","name":"Teutschbein, Valentin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shardul","familyName":"Sapkota","name":"Sapkota, Shardul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sarah","familyName":"Chung","name":"Chung, Sarah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul","familyName":"Schmiedmayer","name":"Schmiedmayer, Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maria Ines","familyName":"Campero","name":"Campero, Maria Ines","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abby C.","familyName":"King","name":"King, Abby C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emma","familyName":"Brunskill","name":"Brunskill, Emma","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James A.","familyName":"Landay","name":"Landay, James A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bloom: Designing for LLM-Augmented Behavior Change Interactions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T23:31:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:15:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-03T22:50:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-05T01:15:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) offer novel opportunities to support health behavior change, yet existing work has narrowly focused on text-only interactions. Building on decades of HCI research on effective behavior change interactions, we present Bloom, an application for physical activity promotion that integrates an LLM-based health coaching chatbot with existing design strategies and UI elements. As part of Bloom's development, we conducted a redteaming evaluation and contribute a safety benchmark dataset. In a four-week randomized field study (N=54) comparing Bloom to a no-LLM control, we observed important shifts in psychological outcomes: participants in the LLM condition reported stronger beliefs that activity was beneficial, greater enjoyment, and more self-compassion. Both conditions significantly increased physical activity levels, doubling the proportion of participants meeting recommended weekly guidelines, though descriptively, we observed no advantage for the LLM condition in short-term physical activity levels. Instead, our findings suggest that LLMs may be more effective at shifting mindsets that precede longer-term behavior change."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05449","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:49:09Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:49:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-05T03:44:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06084","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06084","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06084","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taylor","familyName":"Sorensen","name":"Sorensen, Taylor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Newman","name":"Newman, Benjamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jared","familyName":"Moore","name":"Moore, Jared","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chan","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Chan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jillian","familyName":"Fisher","name":"Fisher, Jillian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niloofar","familyName":"Mireshghallah","name":"Mireshghallah, Niloofar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liwei","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Liwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yejin","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Yejin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spectrum Tuning: Post-Training for Distributional Coverage and In-Context Steerability"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T16:10:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:57:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-03T14:34:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-04T01:56:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Language model post-training has enhanced instruction-following and performance on many downstream tasks, but also comes with an often-overlooked cost on tasks with many possible valid answers. On many tasks such as creative writing, synthetic data generation, or steering to diverse preferences, models must cover an entire distribution of outputs, rather than a single correct answer. We characterize three desiderata for conditional distributional modeling: in-context steerability, valid output space coverage, and distributional alignment, and document across three model families how current post-training can reduce these properties. In particular, we disambiguate between two kinds of in-context learning: ICL for eliciting existing underlying knowledge or capabilities, and in-context steerability, where a model must use in-context information to override its priors and steer to a novel data generating distribution. To better evaluate and improve these desiderata, we introduce Spectrum Suite, a large-scale resource compiled from \u0026gt;40 data sources and spanning \u0026gt;90 tasks requiring models to steer to and match diverse distributions ranging from varied human preferences to numerical distributions and more. We find that while current post-training techniques elicit underlying capabilities and knowledge, they hurt models' ability to flexibly steer in-context. To mitigate these issues, we propose Spectrum Tuning, a post-training method using Spectrum Suite to improve steerability and distributional coverage. We find that Spectrum Tuning often improves over pretrained and typical instruction-tuned models, enhancing steerability, spanning more of the output space, and improving distributional alignment on held-out datasets."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06084","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:04:00Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:04:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-04T03:18:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05684","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05684","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05684","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suhwan","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Suhwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaeyoon","familyName":"Jung","name":"Jung, Jaeyoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haebin","familyName":"Seong","name":"Seong, Haebin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minchan","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Minchan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minyeong","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Minyeong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongjun","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Yongjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoonshik","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Yoonshik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yubeen","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Yubeen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youngjae","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Youngjae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunsung","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Yunsung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"D2E: Scaling Vision-Action Pretraining on Desktop Data for Transfer to Embodied AI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T08:40:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:33:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-17T19:23:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-19T01:05:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T08:55:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-04T01:36:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models leverage internet-scale text data, yet embodied AI remains constrained by the prohibitive costs of physical trajectory collection. Desktop environments -- particularly gaming -- offer a compelling alternative: they provide rich sensorimotor interactions at scale while maintaining the structured observation-action coupling essential for embodied learning. We present D2E (Desktop to Embodied AI), a framework that demonstrates desktop interactions can serve as an effective pretraining substrate for robotics embodied AI tasks. Unlike prior work that remained domain-specific (e.g., VPT for Minecraft) or kept data proprietary (e.g., SIMA), D2E establishes a complete pipeline from scalable desktop data collection to verified transfer in embodied domains. Our framework comprises three components: (1) the OWA Toolkit that unifies diverse desktop interactions into a standardized format with 152x compression, (2) the Generalist-IDM that achieves strong zero-shot generalization across unseen games through timestamp-based event prediction, enabling internet-scale pseudo-labeling, and (3) VAPT that transfers desktop-pretrained representations to physical manipulation and navigation. Using 1.3K+ hours of data (259 hours of human demonstrations and 1K+ hours of pseudo-labeled gameplay), our 1B-parameter model achieves 96.6% success on LIBERO manipulation and 83.3% on CANVAS navigation, matching or surpassing models up to 7x larger, such as π_{0} (3.3B) and OpenVLA (7B). These results demonstrate that sensorimotor primitives learned from digital interactions transfer effectively to real-world physical tasks, establishing desktop pretraining as a practical paradigm for embodied AI. All resources are publicly available at https://worv-ai.github.io/d2e."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05684","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:54:36Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:54:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-04T03:18:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06218","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06218","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06218","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Deheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqian","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Yuqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runyi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Runyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Miao","name":"Miao, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianwen","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Tianwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guolei","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Guolei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ajad","familyName":"Chhatkuli","name":"Chhatkuli, Ajad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanjing","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Xuanjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Gang","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Yu-Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luc","familyName":"Van Gool","name":"Van Gool, Luc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Danda Pani","familyName":"Paudel","name":"Paudel, Danda Pani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EgoNight: Towards Egocentric Vision Understanding at Night with a Challenging Benchmark"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:59:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:04:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-02T11:23:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T03:00:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Most existing benchmarks for understanding egocentric vision focus primarily on daytime scenarios, overlooking the low-light conditions that are inevitable in real-world applications. To investigate this gap, we present EgoNight, the first comprehensive benchmark for nighttime egocentric vision, with visual question answering (VQA) as the core task. A key feature of EgoNight is the introduction of day-night aligned videos, which enhance night annotation quality using the daytime data and reveal clear performance gaps between lighting conditions. To achieve this, we collect both synthetic videos rendered by Blender and real-world recordings, ensuring that scenes and actions are visually and temporally aligned. Leveraging these paired videos, we construct EgoNight-VQA, supported by a novel day-augmented night auto-labeling engine and refinement through extensive human verification. Each QA pair is double-checked by annotators for reliability. In total, EgoNight-VQA contains 3658 QA pairs across 90 videos, spanning 12 diverse QA types, with more than 300 hours of human work. Evaluations of state-of-the-art multimodal large language models (MLLMs) reveal substantial performance drops when transferring from day to night, underscoring the challenges of reasoning under low-light conditions. Beyond VQA, EgoNight also introduces two auxiliary tasks, day-night correspondence retrieval and egocentric depth estimation at night, that further explore the boundaries of existing models. We believe EgoNight-VQA provides a strong foundation for advancing application-driven egocentric vision research and for developing models that generalize across illumination domains. The code and data can be found at https://github.com/dehezhang2/EgoNight."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06218","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:07:09Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:07:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:45:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":310,"totalPages":4,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-07T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-08T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+Transformer+OR+Transformers+OR+%22mixture+of+experts%22+OR+%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22%29\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=1\u0026sort=registered","next":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=2\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-07T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-08T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+Transformer+OR+Transformers+OR+%22mixture+of+experts%22+OR+%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22%29"}}